"use strict";(self.webpackChunkajaymore=self.webpackChunkajaymore||[]).push([["6607"],{7735(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"/2015/12/24/free-basics-illusion","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2015/12/24/free-basics-illusion","source":"@site/blog/2015-12-24-free-basics-illusion/index.md","title":"Free basics illusion","description":"What \u2018free basics\u2019 ideally seeks 1. Digital equity 2. Access to education, jobs, news etc. for billion Indians On digging through facebook\u2019s developer guide I found the following. 1. Facebook\u2019s free basics supports both smart phones and feature phones (read those small screen phones which have virtually unusable browsers) 2. Websites need to be developed\u2026","date":"2015-12-24T11:17:51.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Information Tech","permalink":"/blog/tags/information-tech"},{"inline":true,"label":"net-neutrality","permalink":"/blog/tags/net-neutrality"}],"readingTime":1.03,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Ajay More","title":"Maintainer of ajaymore.in","url":"https://github.com/ajaymore","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7659804?v=4","key":"ajay","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Free basics illusion","date":"2015-12-24T11:17:51.000Z","authors":["ajay"],"tags":["Information Tech","net-neutrality"],"description":"What \u2018free basics\u2019 ideally seeks 1. Digital equity 2. Access to education, jobs, news etc. for billion Indians On digging through facebook\u2019s developer guide I found the following. 1. Facebook\u2019s free basics supports both smart phones and feature phones (read those small screen phones which have virtually unusable browsers) 2. Websites need to be developed\u2026","mdx":{"format":"md"}},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Weekend at Pondicherry","permalink":"/blog/2015/08/05/weekend-at-pondicherry"}},"content":"What \u2018free basics\u2019 ideally seeks  \\n1\\\\. Digital equity  \\n2\\\\. Access to education, jobs, news etc. for billion Indians\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nOn digging through facebook\u2019s developer guide I found the following.  \\n1\\\\. Facebook\u2019s free basics supports both smart phones and feature phones (read those small screen phones which have virtually unusable browsers)  \\n2\\\\. Websites need to be developed in such a way that feature phones are supported, yeah well lets go back to 90s  \\n3\\\\. All the traffic will be routed through internet.org servers so that ISPs can zero rate your service  \\n4\\\\. Facebook can truncate videos, images. Basically morph your site. Read stalking, censorship.\\n\\nNow considering what they are offering and what they want in return the scales are not equal. Free basics is just a medium to get more people online and familiar with Facebook and off course in turn Facebook knows them literally. None of the already shady government sites would work on free basics, talk about fucking digital equity. Free basics is canabalizing the internet and so will others bigshots given a free hand. Forget open standards, open sources. I don\u2019t subscribe to the notion \u201Csomething is better than nothing\u201D there are always costs, costs we are not aware of and that is even worse."},{"id":"/2015/08/05/weekend-at-pondicherry","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2015/08/05/weekend-at-pondicherry","source":"@site/blog/2015-08-05-weekend-at-pondicherry/index.md","title":"Weekend at Pondicherry","description":"Pondicherry is a union territory of India with the city of Pondicherry as its capital. The city fondly called Pondy is a coastal city with a mix of different ethnicities, architectures. The city is well planned and some of its areas are simply mesmerizing in terms of its beauty. I visited the place recently with a\u2026","date":"2015-08-05T05:55:54.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Trek & Travel","permalink":"/blog/tags/trek-travel"},{"inline":true,"label":"Architecture","permalink":"/blog/tags/architecture"},{"inline":true,"label":"beach","permalink":"/blog/tags/beach"},{"inline":true,"label":"City","permalink":"/blog/tags/city"},{"inline":true,"label":"travel","permalink":"/blog/tags/travel"}],"readingTime":5.4,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Ajay More","title":"Maintainer of ajaymore.in","url":"https://github.com/ajaymore","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7659804?v=4","key":"ajay","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Weekend at Pondicherry","date":"2015-08-05T05:55:54.000Z","authors":["ajay"],"tags":["Trek & Travel","Architecture","beach","City","travel"],"description":"Pondicherry is a union territory of India with the city of Pondicherry as its capital. The city fondly called Pondy is a coastal city with a mix of different ethnicities, architectures. The city is well planned and some of its areas are simply mesmerizing in terms of its beauty. I visited the place recently with a\u2026","image":"./img_20150801_083418.jpg","mdx":{"format":"md"}},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Free basics illusion","permalink":"/blog/2015/12/24/free-basics-illusion"},"nextItem":{"title":"Trek to Kunti Betta, Karnataka","permalink":"/blog/2015/07/28/trek-to-kunti-betta-karnataka"}},"content":"Pondicherry is a union territory of India with the city of Pondicherry as its capital. The city fondly called Pondy is a coastal city with a mix of different ethnicities, architectures. The city is well planned and some of its areas are simply mesmerizing in terms of its beauty. I visited the place\xa0recently with a friend of mine.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nWe started our journey to Pondicherry from Bangalore on the night of 31st July 2015. The bus journey started around 2300Hrs in the night and ended 8 hours later in the morning around 0730Hrs. took an auto rickshaw to a place called New Guest House. The guest house is a religious place so you aren\u2019t allowed to drink or smoke and need to be back before 2230Hrs in the night. If that\u2019s fine with you then it\u2019s the place of your stay in Pondicherry for several reasons. First it\u2019s just 50mtrs away from the promenade beach and at the edge of the most famous white town area of Pondy. Secondly it\u2019s pretty cheap considering the locality as it costs only 400 rupees for a single night stay, and being affiliated to religious organization it\u2019s neat and tidy. We checked in, got refreshed and came out on the promenade beach for a refreshingly beautiful morning walk. Beaches are the most serene places to be at in the mornings.\\n\\nWe had a breakfast at a road side joint, the street food in Pondy is quite cheaper and won\u2019t burn into your pockets. We decided to take an auto to a place called Paradise beach also famously called Boat House. The autos in Pondy don\u2019t make use of the meters and the drivers will let you know the fare firsthand. The self-fixed prices of the autos are quite steep and will remain so even after a hard bargain, sometimes you can\u2019t even bargain. Alternately the public transport in Pondicherry is almost free, it costs you just 5 rupees from any place to place. So make it a point to lookout for public buses or ask around. Sometime you won\u2019t have a choice and would need to use the auto.\\n\\n![IMG_20150801_102408](./img_20150801_102408.jpg)\\n\\nParadise beach is sort of an island beach and requires a boat ride to reach. The beach is clean, no litter in the vicinity with lots of photogenic spaces.\\n\\n![IMG_20150801_101057](./img_20150801_101057.jpg)  \\nWe visited a place called Arikamedu in the afternoon, it used to be a Roman trading hub in the ancient times. There is not much to see there and you should visit only if you are a true history enthusiast. In this part of the Pondy you will find simple Tamil style homes and is a part of Pondy that is often less talked of.\\n\\n![IMG_20150801_122029](./img_20150801_122029.jpg)\\n\\n![Relics from the first century](./img_20150801_125030.jpg)\\n*Relics from the first century*\\n\\nWe were back to the guest house by late afternoon and decided to explore Pondy markets after a brief rest. We had some street food at Chinna Kadai market, then walked from street to street. There are specialized streets in the city, they give you a glimpse into the planning of the city. One should explore all the streets around MG road area like Nehru Street, Mission Street and many others. Also give a visit to Arulmigu Manakula temple. There are also quite a many churches in the city. You can also find Aurobindo Ashram near the same area. The city looks pretty amazing in the night. The walk along the Promenade beach in the night will make you spellbound, the French windows just look majestic at night. Sit for some long time at the beach and listen to the sound of the waves and observe the people around.\\n\\n![IMG_20150801_171310](./img_20150801_171310.jpg)\\n![IMG_20150801_173418~3](./img_20150801_1734183.jpg)\\n![IMG_20150801_201228](./img_20150801_201228.jpg)\\n![IMG_20150801_210350](./img_20150801_210350.jpg)\\n\\nWe checked out the next day from the guest house. Took a bus to the New city bus stand from the market near Pondicherry railway station. Got into another bus towards Auroville. The bus drops you at the main road leading to Auroville, however the places to visit in Auroville are about 8kms from there. Through some miscalculation we didn\u2019t take an auto rickshaw to the Auroville town and ended up walking 8kms.\\n\\nAuroville is an experimental township, it\u2019s an international community undivided by ideas of nationality, religion or ethnicity. Since we walked our way we got to see the place in all its glory. The place is one big man made forest. The architecture of the place is awe inspiring and will make you promise yourself that you will come back for more number of days to stay here.\\n\\n![IMG_20150802_125927](./img_20150802_125927.jpg)\\n![IMG_20150802_122247](./img_20150802_122247.jpg)\\n![IMG_20150802_120753](./img_20150802_120753.jpg)\\n![IMG_20150802_110755](./img_20150802_110755.jpg)\\n![IMG_20150802_100746](./img_20150802_100746.jpg)\\n\\nTook an auto from the Auroville back to the main road, visited Auroville beach a walkable distance from there. Had some fun at the beach, there were few absolutely drunk college students at the beach and had an opportunity to photograph one.\\n\\n![IMG_20150802_152621](./img_20150802_152621.jpg)\\n\\nBack from the beach and back to the city bus stand, we visited Pondicherry botanical garden, it\u2019s more of a park that botanical garden. We spent last few hours in the city at the Promenade beach. I had some really creative thoughts emerging in my mind as I stared at the endless ocean.\\n\\nPondicherry is a city to visit, for its architecture, for its culture and for its simplicity. It\u2019s not a perfect city and there is a lot that its people and infrastructure can improve upon. I found one thing very disturbing, the chief minister of the territory was on an image making spree in the city. There were these huge poster of Mr. N. Rangaswamy flanking every corner of the city. However amusing the posters might be, the sight was plain ugly. On another aspect the city has a lot of open sewers, the smaller sewers should be covered at the very least. However I liked the fact that people were quite laid back and didn\u2019t seem to be in a lot of hurry like in the cities of Mumbai, Pune or Bangalore.\\n\\n[View photo album](https://goo.gl/photos/qhSGuBGQn5EnmJoW7)\\n\\nThe map of the places to explore.\\n\\n[https://www.google.com/maps/d/edit?mid=zNGkMTWtKgM4.kaMfL\\\\_7jo\\\\_ms&usp=sharing](https://www.google.com/maps/d/edit?mid=zNGkMTWtKgM4.kaMfL_7jo_ms&usp=sharing)  \\n\\n<iframe src=\\"https://www.google.com/maps/d/embed?mid=zNGkMTWtKgM4.kaMfL_7jo_ms\\" width=\\"640\\" height=\\"480\\" style=\\"max-width: 100%; border: 0;\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" allowfullscreen></iframe>"},{"id":"/2015/07/28/trek-to-kunti-betta-karnataka","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2015/07/28/trek-to-kunti-betta-karnataka","source":"@site/blog/2015-07-28-trek-to-kunti-betta-karnataka/index.md","title":"Trek to Kunti Betta, Karnataka","description":"There is something about trekking that enlivens your senses, releases the tensions within; both physical and mental. It\u2019s an ordeal into achieving nothing. Many of my friends find it hard to follow the reasons why I trek. I hardly care about the summit or the destination. For me the journey itself holds the charm above all\u2026","date":"2015-07-28T11:10:53.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Trek & Travel","permalink":"/blog/tags/trek-travel"},{"inline":true,"label":"adventure","permalink":"/blog/tags/adventure"},{"inline":true,"label":"trains","permalink":"/blog/tags/trains"},{"inline":true,"label":"trek","permalink":"/blog/tags/trek"}],"readingTime":3.73,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Ajay More","title":"Maintainer of ajaymore.in","url":"https://github.com/ajaymore","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7659804?v=4","key":"ajay","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Trek to Kunti Betta, Karnataka","date":"2015-07-28T11:10:53.000Z","authors":["ajay"],"tags":["Trek & Travel","adventure","trains","trek"],"description":"There is something about trekking that enlivens your senses, releases the tensions within; both physical and mental. It\u2019s an ordeal into achieving nothing. Many of my friends find it hard to follow the reasons why I trek. I hardly care about the summit or the destination. For me the journey itself holds the charm above all\u2026","image":"./img_20150726_105502_1.jpg","mdx":{"format":"md"}},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Weekend at Pondicherry","permalink":"/blog/2015/08/05/weekend-at-pondicherry"},"nextItem":{"title":"Chavand, Shivneri Trek","permalink":"/blog/2014/06/28/chavand-shivneri-trek"}},"content":"There is something about trekking that enlivens your\xa0senses, releases the tensions within; both physical and mental. It\u2019s an ordeal into achieving nothing. Many of my friends find it hard to follow the reasons why I trek. I hardly care about the summit or the destination. For me the journey itself holds the charm above all else. Okay so all philosophy apart, I write this blog post to share my experiences of travelling to a place called Kunti Betta 100 km away from Bangalore.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nWoke up at 0445 hours on the sunday, 26th July 15. Being the organizer had to call 7 more people to make sure they were awake. We were suppose to leave the hostel by 0530 hours, but somehow someone will always find some excuse to get you late. \u{1F642} So we started 20 minutes late. Reached Hosa Road Junction by 0600 Hours and reached the Majestic Bus station at 0640 hours. We had to hurry to the adjacent railway station almost running en route. Got the tickets and boarded the train bound for Mysore at 0700 hours. Our destination\xa0was Pandavpura, the place is believed to have been the stay of the Pandav from Mahabharata during their exile.\\n\\n![Train to Pandavpura](./img_20150726_075844_1.jpg)\\n*Train to Pandavpura*\\n\\nThe morning train journey was surreal, the smoky train engine kept me interested until the end. We reached the place around 0930 hours. Walked out of the railway station and searched for a place to have breakfast. Pandavpura town is about 4 km away from the railway station. If you wish to have\xa0your breakfast at a neat and clean place\xa0then area near the station is not a place for you. We looked out for a place to eat but no eating joint matched our idea of a good place. Finally we settled for one less shady looking joint\xa0and asked for breakfast options. The place served Rice(Pulav), idly, udid-vada and puri. The prices of the menu-items\xa0were way to cheap. We ordered 3 rice plates, 2 idli-vada plates and started eating. The food was so freaking awesome and tasty we ended up eating like 6 rice plates, 4 idli-vada plates, 1 Puri-sabji. And to add to our delight we paid only 220 rupees for all that food. Sometimes unfamiliar and not so good looking places have the best things to offer. :D.\\n\\nWe negotiated with an ambassador taxi driver to take us to Kunti betta, 8 of us squeezed ourselves in the royal ambassador and drove off for Kunti Betta. The photos below will describe the beauty of the place and I would let you imagine the words. Just to mention, the place might get tricky to climb if it rains, that\u2019s all.\\n\\n![IMG_20150726_112005_1](./img_20150726_112005_1.jpg)\\n![IMG_20150726_101000_1](./img_20150726_101000_1.jpg)\\n![IMG_20150726_103218_1](./img_20150726_103218_1.jpg)\\n![IMG_20150726_104126_1](./img_20150726_104126_1.jpg)\\n![IMG_20150726_105502_1](./img_20150726_105502_1.jpg)\\n![IMG_20150726_105455_1](./img_20150726_105455_1.jpg)\\n\\nAfter climbing down we started off for Tonnur lake some 15km away. The lake is super clean, we reached from the point where people enter the lake for a swim. Had lots of photos taken; diving, jumping and shouting. We met with another travelling group from Karnataka at the lake. The guys spoke broken Hindi and English, but somehow we got along very well. They offered us delicious chicken and rice to eat. They really liked the fact that 8 of us came from 7 different states in India. After a final photograph with them we headed back to Pandavpura railway station. We just made it in time for 1350 hours train back to Bangalore. There were no seats available so had to squeeze ourselves onto the upper birth where one\xa0couldn\u2019t sit with his\xa0head and spine erect. After one and half hour of acrobatics on the upper birth where I\xa0also managed to get some decent amount sleep, I got down and spent the rest of the time at the train door \u2013 my most favorite spot in any train.\\n\\n![Thonnur Lake](./img_20150726_125733_1.jpg)\\n\\n![New friends](./img_0734.jpg)\\n\\nWatched the railway track side ghettos of Bangalore while getting back felt a bit sad as to why people are forced to abandon\xa0clean environments of the villages to settle in such stinking, sunlight deprived places and to what avail? Below is the map of the place, tracing the route from Pandavpura railway station.\\n\\n<iframe src=\\"https://www.google.com/maps/d/embed?mid=zNGkMTWtKgM4.kItKq6FH6lTA\\" width=\\"640\\" height=\\"480\\" style=\\"max-width: 100%; border: 0;\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" allowfullscreen></iframe>"},{"id":"/2014/06/28/chavand-shivneri-trek","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2014/06/28/chavand-shivneri-trek","source":"@site/blog/2014-06-28-chavand-shivneri-trek/index.md","title":"Chavand, Shivneri Trek","description":"The monsoon has been delayed this year, however the weather seems much cooler now in comparison to summer. Perfect time to come out of trekking hibernation. Last Saturday we planned a trek to Chavand and Shivneri, two hill forts situated in Pune district. We started early morning around 6am from Pune and reached the destination\u2026","date":"2014-06-28T07:39:45.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Trek & Travel","permalink":"/blog/tags/trek-travel"},{"inline":true,"label":"heritage","permalink":"/blog/tags/heritage"}],"readingTime":4.07,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Ajay More","title":"Maintainer of ajaymore.in","url":"https://github.com/ajaymore","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7659804?v=4","key":"ajay","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Chavand, Shivneri Trek","date":"2014-06-28T07:39:45.000Z","authors":["ajay"],"tags":["Trek & Travel","heritage"],"description":"The monsoon has been delayed this year, however the weather seems much cooler now in comparison to summer. Perfect time to come out of trekking hibernation. Last Saturday we planned a trek to Chavand and Shivneri, two hill forts situated in Pune district. We started early morning around 6am from Pune and reached the destination\u2026","image":"./5.jpg","mdx":{"format":"md"}},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Trek to Kunti Betta, Karnataka","permalink":"/blog/2015/07/28/trek-to-kunti-betta-karnataka"},"nextItem":{"title":"The art of programming","permalink":"/blog/2014/04/22/the-art-of-programming"}},"content":"The monsoon has been delayed this year, however the weather seems much cooler now in comparison to summer. Perfect time to come out of trekking hibernation. Last Saturday we planned a trek to Chavand and Shivneri, two hill forts situated in Pune district. We started early morning around 6am from Pune and reached the destination around 7:45 am. Had a breakfast of tasty Misal at a small roadside hotel.\xa0\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nThe base village of Chavand is a quiet and beautiful place. The houses in village are traditional with roofs mainly of roof tiles and metal sheets. There is a single main road that passes through the entire village to end of which there is a primary school. We parked our car in the primary school playground. Filled our water bottles and asked locals for directions to the fort and places of interest to be seen atop the fort.\\n\\n![3](./3.jpg)\\n\\n![4](./4.jpg)\\n\\nThe climb to the fort is not a difficult one, currently work is in progress for creating steps all the way to the top. The fort itself is a simple plateau without huge walls and big bastions. What\u2019s more remarkable about the place is the restoration effort carried out by government with assistance from locals. It\u2019s clear from one look that all money spent here in maintenance hasn\u2019t been a waste. Although not a major tourist attraction still, there are enough sign boards in place. Most requesting tourist to maintain the sanctity of the place. There are thousands of tree saplings planted and cared for, in a few years\u2019 time, visit to this place will be more of ecological one than archaeological. We all have a lot to learn from the simple village folks here, primarily heritage and forest conservation. There are remains of ancient Shiva temple on the fort which reminds you of the wonderful ancient art and architecture. To me it seemed straight out of an Indiana jones movie \u{1F642} .\\n\\n![1](./1.jpg) ![2](./2.jpg)\\n\\nThe sky looked amazing with dark rainy clouds, however we missed the most anticipated rain. It would have been an icing on the cake. Having strolled around for some time, we descended the fort. At the foot of the fort met some village children who had picked two small plastic bagful of jambul (Java plum) fruits. They wished to sell those, shyly one asked if we would buy? We happily bought all the fruits they had, they would have given us the entire fetch for whatever we were ready to pay, but we decided to stick to our morals and give them a fair return. Back to our car, played bit of cricket\xa0with village children. Got refreshed at a well nearby drawing water using a bucket tied to rope. Once freshened up we started back to the fort Shivneri. It\u2019s hardly a 20-30 min drive from Chavand.\\n\\n![6](./6.jpg)\\n\\nShivneri is birthplace of Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj, the founder of Maratha Empire. The place holds a good deal of reverence in my heart. It\u2019s heartening to see lots of restoration work underway on this fort. However call me a skeptic but I do not fully appreciate the nature of restoration work carried out around most forts in Maharashtra. Restoration workers either have no idea of the aesthetic value of the place they are restoring or they are hardly bothered. The same is the case with contractors who are hired to do the job. Archaeological survey teams who can actually make justice to the restoration process are poorly numbered and cannot supervise every small activity. Still even use of simple common sense, and an effort at letting the originality shine and the patchwork be there to support the beauty and not obstruct it will make a great difference.\\n\\n![7](./7.jpg)\\n\\nLike Pratapgad, Shivneri isn\u2019t much of a trek but a long walk. Many beautiful structures still remain intact thanks to restoration work. There are enough spots for lots of photography. Green cover too on the fort is excellent and provides for a wonderful shade for weary tourists. The fort is huge and takes a good amount of time to cover up. It\xa0holds many still intact water tanks, a huge lake, few palaces and gateways. It does succeed in reminding you of the\xa0glorious past.\\n\\n![8](./8.jpg)\\n\\nBack to the base, \xa0we started for\xa0Pune. Had a light lunch at a roadside hotel on the way. Were back to the city by 6pm.\\n\\nEvery time I return from a trek and the next day arrives, I always feel rejuvenated, the feeling is of having hit the reset button on all the unnecessary clutter created in life. Makes me reiterate a quote I found on this blog. [http://www.scoopwhoop.com/inothernews/travel-changes-you/](http://www.scoopwhoop.com/inothernews/travel-changes-you/)\\n\\n> With every trip you take, you become just a little wiser."},{"id":"/2014/04/22/the-art-of-programming","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2014/04/22/the-art-of-programming","source":"@site/blog/2014-04-22-the-art-of-programming/index.md","title":"The art of programming","description":"Software development might be a science but an excellent code is a work of art. The programmer uses a language to write code, which has its own grammar. The programmer follows the syntax, composes accurate expressions and does his best to create concise code. As with literature only mere combination of right words and sentences\u2026","date":"2014-04-22T11:36:20.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Information Tech","permalink":"/blog/tags/information-tech"},{"inline":true,"label":"programming","permalink":"/blog/tags/programming"}],"readingTime":2.55,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Ajay More","title":"Maintainer of ajaymore.in","url":"https://github.com/ajaymore","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7659804?v=4","key":"ajay","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"The art of programming","date":"2014-04-22T11:36:20.000Z","authors":["ajay"],"tags":["Information Tech","programming"],"description":"Software development might be a science but an excellent code is a work of art. The programmer uses a language to write code, which has its own grammar. The programmer follows the syntax, composes accurate expressions and does his best to create concise code. As with literature only mere combination of right words and sentences\u2026","image":"./ctwa0v1.png","mdx":{"format":"md"}},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Chavand, Shivneri Trek","permalink":"/blog/2014/06/28/chavand-shivneri-trek"},"nextItem":{"title":"Where is my weekend?","permalink":"/blog/2014/04/13/where-is-my-weekend"}},"content":"Software development might be a science but an excellent code is a work of art. The programmer uses a language to write code, which has its own grammar. The programmer follows the syntax, composes accurate expressions and does his best to create concise code. As with literature only mere combination of right words and sentences does not produce a beautiful poem or prose, similarly just an act of writing correct code does not make it aesthetic or pleasing.\xa0\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nAlthough written to achieve an objective, program itself is a subjective entity. The code is a reflection of the programmers\u2019 personality. Good programmers get emotionally involved with their code, programs are often described using words like ugly, beautiful, awesome or sexy. There is a kind of personal bond with the piece of code. Names of movies, movie characters, ice-creams, donuts, birds, animals, trees, flowers and many other day to day nouns are commonly found\xa0as names of software. Programmers are very close to these things from real life and hence they choose to name their software after them. A good programmer relies greatly on intuition rather than memory.\\n\\nProgramming seeks to create a two way interaction, which is a step up from traditional art forms. The users can not only appreciate the beauty of the end result but also play with it. Like an artist programmer derives inspiration from his surroundings and seeks to imitate them. It\u2019s the same reason why today\u2019s interfaces are so more intuitive and quick to learn. People can easily understand the purpose of an object by merely looking at it. It\u2019s no surprise why we have terms like bins, folders, files, note-pads, forms, buttons, drag & drop, click, tap, swipe, eBooks, calendars, drawers and many more used commonly in interface design.\\n\\nLike atoms in the real world, pixel is the building block of life on screen. Screen gives life to the character. We can program feelings of pain, sorrow, misery by way of pixel manipulation. Almost every real world phenomenon can be simulated in software environment. So be it the universe, solar system, the gravity or the sub-atomic world. Off course we cannot create or impart consciousness to our characters from the virtual world, even if we build physical robots that still doesn\u2019t seem to be the possibility. But with programming we can come unbelievably close to creating the illusion of reality, which at times isn\u2019t illusion at all. Try playing a game like Prince of Persia or Counter strike and see how you respond to the character\u2019s action. Programming gives you the ability to play the role of the creator where your imaginations have no bounds.\\n\\nIf one could learn his spoken language, then the person can learn a programming language too. Like any other language it takes practice and frequent use. Children benefit greatly from learning to program. Programming opens up a whole new world for these kids. For adults there is more than enough in the programming world to provide you with a hobby for a lifetime, whose benefits are incalculable."},{"id":"/2014/04/13/where-is-my-weekend","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2014/04/13/where-is-my-weekend","source":"@site/blog/2014-04-13-where-is-my-weekend/index.md","title":"Where is my weekend?","description":"Currently there is an epidemic that has gripped the IT industry. The epidemic called weekend working. The disease that has left thousands stressful, depressed and unhappy. There is a huge number of smart, hardworking individuals who are exposed to this new way of slave labour. People are forced to forgo the desire to bond with\u2026","date":"2014-04-13T11:07:36.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Work","permalink":"/blog/tags/work"},{"inline":true,"label":"ethics","permalink":"/blog/tags/ethics"}],"readingTime":7.25,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Ajay More","title":"Maintainer of ajaymore.in","url":"https://github.com/ajaymore","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7659804?v=4","key":"ajay","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Where is my weekend?","date":"2014-04-13T11:07:36.000Z","authors":["ajay"],"tags":["Work","ethics"],"description":"Currently there is an epidemic that has gripped the IT industry. The epidemic called weekend working. The disease that has left thousands stressful, depressed and unhappy. There is a huge number of smart, hardworking individuals who are exposed to this new way of slave labour. People are forced to forgo the desire to bond with\u2026","image":"./6a00d83420a27c53ef00e550676b738833-800wi.png","mdx":{"format":"md"}},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"The art of programming","permalink":"/blog/2014/04/22/the-art-of-programming"},"nextItem":{"title":"10 pounds lighter","permalink":"/blog/2014/03/21/10-pounds-lighter"}},"content":"Currently there is an epidemic that has gripped the IT industry. The epidemic called weekend working. The disease that has left thousands stressful, depressed and unhappy. There is a huge number of smart, hardworking individuals who are exposed to this new way of slave labour. People are forced to forgo the desire to bond with their families, people are coerced to give up their priorities in life and focus on only single objective: saving their only job. There are numerous disgruntled employees who prefer countless hours of gossip at their desks instead of few productive hours of actual work. This may not be the case at every organization out there, but potentially it\u2019s *\u2018*THE CASE*\u2018* at every organization. When things go wrong weekend work is the first solution that appears. Nobody thinks it is unethical to take away people\u2019s moral right to get rest and rejuvenate themselves. Managers instill a sense of guilt into the minds of employees from the very beginning of the project, virtually scaring them off with warnings like: \u201Cnobody likes to work on a weekend, you guys better complete your tasks on time and we will never call you over the weekend\u201D. So if at all people are required to work weekends, it\u2019s their own fault as they did not give proper attention to the manager\u2019s prophecy.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nYes I agree, there might be a critical situation where weekend work is the only solution. But if not defined within legal framework or as a matter of policy, then the critical situation becomes a matter of perspective. A weak project manager who is easily scared by the client will turn almost any adversity into a critical matter. I have seen people work weekends at almost all phases of software development. The criticality of the situation is almost always overblown. Weekend work is an easy excuse of the offshore managers for their incompetency. It gives them an argument that says \u201CI am getting my team to work 10-11 hours a day, I am getting them to work over weekends. What more do you expect of me. Things are difficult here, there is very little that is in my hands.\u201D The managers themselves are typical victims of this viciousness. There is no tyranny here, everyone suffers equally. Be it the manager or the employee. The manager\u2019s have to save their job too. The higher management cares very little, they often think people down the hierarchy are usually lazy and do not understand the gravity of the situation. There is wrong sense of pride in upper management who firmly believe that it\u2019s their interfacing with the client that brings million dollar projects, and it\u2019s the job of managers and developers to complete the project in stipulated time, let whatever be the difficulty. I totally agree with the first part, but it\u2019s the second part that bothers me. Let whatever be the difficulty! This thinking has given rise to a sort of moral degradation which eventually results in forcing people to work for 2-3 weeks without a day of rest.\\n\\nI classify the IT working population into 5 categories based on their likeliness to agree over working a weekend.\\n\\n1.  Freshers (New joinees)\\n2.  \xa0People with vested interest (onsite deputation, promotions, good grades)\\n3.  The gullible, people who cannot say no to authority.\\n4.  The responsible (Married, debt ridden etc.)\\n5.  Free spirits. (The I don\u2019t give a damn types)\\n\\nThe responsible and the free spirits are the only ones who oppose the system of weekend work. The rest are either confused or leave it for others to fight, they are okay either ways. It\u2019s a classic psychology game. If anyone agrees to work over a weekend, then the managers use their example to coerce others. If a junior agrees then a senior is obliged as a sense of responsibility. If a senior agrees then junior has to agree as who knows better than a senior. Managers are pretty good at this game. Few who oppose cannot do so forever as they start suffering from [cognitive dissonance](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_dissonance), Their rational minds asks them to change their opinion by giving reasons of responsibility, obligation, guilt, team work and what not. It\u2019s really difficult to take a stand that you won\u2019t work under unethical conditions when all about you have given up. The major blame for weekend work culture lies on the individuals, who are either too naive to think about their own well-being or are to messed up in the responsibilities of the life. Either way we don\u2019t understand, what constitutes a good work ethic. Nobody talks about weekend work as a matter of right and wrong but instead confuse it with unwarranted situation.\\n\\nThe strong motive for writing this article comes from the psychological and physical impact weekend work has on the minds of the employees.\\n\\n1.  Weekend work sends a message into the subconscious of individuals that their life is not in their control, they are overworked, and do not have time for simple pleasures of life. Sometimes even though they have plenty of time, they cannot muster up enough energy to get rid of the inertia created by those senseless visits to the office for no reason.\\n2.  People who work weekends rarely do any kind of physical activity during those times. As such people prefer not to workout even when their lives are okay. Weekend workers don\u2019t feel guilty for abusing their body since they feel trapped and lost. The thought of exercise appears ridiculous compared to the ordeal one is facing. Thus they compromise their health, which might lead to serious disease in near\xa0foreseeable\xa0future.\\n3.  People become absolutely inefficient and notoriously gossipy at work. I will bet more time is spent on gossip than actual work. That\u2019s the only creative haven they can resort to and employees use it in all perfection to get rid of their daily stress.\\n4.  There is a change in attitude weekend work instils a strong dislike towards the organization, superiors and in lots of cases dislike towards the work which is a major blow to person\u2019s individual growth.\\n5.  Family life is disturbed. People cannot take time off to take care of their loved ones. There are cases where people get called from hospitals, when their loved ones are in intensive care. There are plenty of examples where newly married couples have had the worst times of their life when their marriage just got started.\\n6.  While you work on weekends there are no plans, no recreational leaves, you are no more master of your life. The very tenets of freedom are killed right there. And nobody makes any fuss about it.\\n\\nLike many of my colleagues I am not aware of labour laws. Did a bit of googling, but couldn\u2019t find laws that address this hairy issue.  \\nCompanies provide an alternative week off by way of their policies towards a weekend worked. But that\u2019s not right as people volunteer (although not true \u{1F61B} ) to give up their time of comfort, in order to address the difficult times the project is facing. So they should be paid in a better fashion. The irresponsible company in one case doesn\u2019t even manage the transportation of the employee, and the individual has to cough up his own money to work over weekends, which is more humiliating. Besides thrifty remuneration, the week off itself isn\u2019t any good. I have seen people accumulate week offs to the count of 12-15 in a span of 4 months. Which is a half of their total weekends during that duration. In those times they do not get to enjoy their own paid leaves or public holidays even. There is a blatant abuse of law. People have worked on national holidays and Labor Day at times. What\u2019s more concerning is that the employee accumulates all these leaves which he cannot take and they eventually lapse off, unless off course somebody is allowed to take a big long vacation, which again is a rare case.\\n\\nSome people may prefer good remuneration for working over weekends or working extra hours at office, as is the case in developed countries. But if you ask me I will say it\u2019s a slice of your precious life that you offer for nothing. Our jobs are means to an end, in case they are not what we wanted them to be. But our time away from that place of work is our opportunity to be better family members, better citizens, better intellectuals and better friends. It\u2019s that time of leisure which makes us more interesting person, if we are missing on that then what life are we leading?"},{"id":"/2014/03/21/10-pounds-lighter","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2014/03/21/10-pounds-lighter","source":"@site/blog/2014-03-21-10-pounds-lighter/index.md","title":"10 pounds lighter","description":"Regular exercise and ability to do heavy physical activity whenever required wasn\u2019t enough to keep away that excess fat around my belly. My diet is moderately regulated, but sometimes it just goes berserk, chocolates and sweets are my greatest adversaries when it comes to losing weight. A hundred times I had tried dieting and failed.\u2026","date":"2014-03-20T18:53:48.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Health","permalink":"/blog/tags/health"},{"inline":true,"label":"Diet","permalink":"/blog/tags/diet"},{"inline":true,"label":"self control","permalink":"/blog/tags/self-control"},{"inline":true,"label":"weight loss","permalink":"/blog/tags/weight-loss"}],"readingTime":5.71,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Ajay More","title":"Maintainer of ajaymore.in","url":"https://github.com/ajaymore","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7659804?v=4","key":"ajay","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"10 pounds lighter","date":"2014-03-20T18:53:48.000Z","authors":["ajay"],"tags":["Health","Diet","self control","weight loss"],"description":"Regular exercise and ability to do heavy physical activity whenever required wasn\u2019t enough to keep away that excess fat around my belly. My diet is moderately regulated, but sometimes it just goes berserk, chocolates and sweets are my greatest adversaries when it comes to losing weight. A hundred times I had tried dieting and failed.\u2026","mdx":{"format":"md"}},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Where is my weekend?","permalink":"/blog/2014/04/13/where-is-my-weekend"},"nextItem":{"title":"Atheism 2.0","permalink":"/blog/2014/03/19/atheism-2-0"}},"content":"Regular exercise and ability to do heavy physical activity whenever required wasn\u2019t enough to keep away that excess fat around my belly. My diet is moderately regulated, but sometimes it just goes berserk, chocolates and sweets are my greatest adversaries when it comes to losing weight. A hundred times I had tried dieting and failed. It\u2019s not that dieting never worked, but the thing was I had such great expectations, that I was stubborn to have it all or nothing. So whenever I hit a plateau I just quit. Recently it became so worse that I just quit the effort of keeping my weight under check. And as expected this had a dire effect. With weeks and months passing by my weight kept on mounting. The weight that fluctuated between 68-70kg, was now 73 and it didn\u2019t feel right.\xa0\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nBack from a trip to Goa, I was quite pumped up to lose that extra flab and feel good about myself. Received some very useful tips from my close friends and felt the time was right to just do it. Below is my journey of four weeks that helped me lose more than 10 pounds about 5kg to be more exact. The only thing different this time was that I had better tools, better control and better knowledge of my situation.\\n\\n> Week 1  \\n> First week is usually neutral. There is enough will power in reserve, and although body and mind provides a good deal of resistance the will power counter balances it.\\n\\nDiet:  \\nBreakfast: 1 raw egg, oatmeal, green tea.  \\nLunch: 1/2 chicken tandoori or sprout-vegetable salad.  \\nEve-snacks: omelette with brown bread  \\nDinner: sprout salad\\n\\nSupplements : 1 vitamin-mineral capsule (Revital) after dinner.\\n\\nWorkout:  \\nBody weight exercises in the morning, with focus on upper and lower body on alternate days.  \\n20-30min walk after dinner.\\n\\nLifestyle changes:  \\nMeditation 10min, morning and evening.  \\nA good 8 hour sleep.\\n\\nChallenges:  \\nConvincing friends, colleagues, family of my intentions, and confronting the ensuing embarrassment. There is not a soul in the world except for you who thinks dieting would be successful.  \\nWeekends normally ruin your resolve, I chose to stay vigilant. I exercised moderately on weekends to remind myself of the goal ahead.\\n\\n> Week 2  \\n> I could already see changes happening, I did feel lighter. Overall lethargy of the body was reduced to a great extent. Muscles felt more in control. The Monday felt awesome.\\n\\nDiet changes:  \\nEve-snacks: omelette was replaced with no-sugar fruit juice. I freaked over omelette being fried & oily, thus me consuming more than necessary calories.  \\nDinner: 2 spoon Apple cider juice before sleep, apple cider is known for reducing obesity, I thought of giving it a try.\\n\\nWorkout changes:  \\nSwimming after office hours for about half an hour.\\n\\nLifestyle changes:  \\nMeditation 15min, morning and evening.\\n\\nChallenges:  \\nAvoiding those innocent moments where someone offers you a chocolate or a pastry or some other sweet. Sometimes I felt I was being too conscious, but it helped. I didn\u2019t fall prey. I knew I wouldn\u2019t last for long if this continued, but I wanted to fight it out as long as I could.\\n\\n> Week 3  \\n> The weight had reduced considerably. However I felt very low on my overall energy level. It was becoming increasingly difficult to improve my strength training routine. I always felt short of inspiration and energy. Also I had hit the plateau, most visible fat had reduced and it was difficult to spot improvement by just looking at the mirror. The week was long and arduous. However the results were incredible. By the weeks\u2019 end I had made a considerable progress.\\n\\nDiet changes:  \\nEating nuts almonds/walnuts whenever possible throughout the day.  \\nEve-snacks: eating almonds, no fruit juice.  \\nDinner: 2 spoon peanut butter with sprouts.\\n\\nWorkout changes:  \\nDaily walk of 30min after dinner.\\n\\nLifestyle changes:  \\nMeditation 20min, morning and evening.\\n\\nChallenges:  \\nI was considered for a serious project work which demanded more time, and more focused work. This new task drained a lot of vital energy and I felt exhausted on almost all days. Sometimes I would straight away go to sleep after coming from office so that I didn\u2019t indulge myself on happy food due to lack of energy. Once I had a nice nap of about 20-30 min. I would wake up and go about the tasks remaining for the day.\\n\\n> Week 4  \\n> I knew very well that my diet has to end, and it should end on a happy note. I shouldn\u2019t regret it being broken. There shouldn\u2019t be any feeling of guilt when it broke. So I had to gradually switch to eating normal. Also indulge at times but with total awareness of where I was headed to. This week I was merrier as I felt privileged to try stuff that was forbidden for 3 weeks. Now I could observe myself being conscious all the time about whatever went inside my stomach. I was polite in refusing, whenever some delicacy was given due indulgence and it was time to stop.\\n\\nDiet changes:  \\nBreakfast: 1 raw egg, oatmeal, green tea, with freedom to try any other on alternate days.  \\nLunch: 1/2 chicken tandoori or sprout-vegetable salad or any other meal that is just about enough and did not make me feel bloated.  \\nEve-snacks: omelette with brown bread  \\nDinner: normal meal, no rice.\\n\\nWorkout changes:  \\nStrength training in morning, brisk walk in the evening.\\n\\nLifestyle changes:  \\nMeditation 10min, morning and evening.\\n\\n> Closing note:  \\n> Dieting is challenging stuff, but we should aspire to see the change in ourselves. Everyone\u2019s body type is unique and experimentation is the key. Experiment, observe and make changes, repeat this process for as long as is possible until you are sure what works best for you. It\u2019s a wonderful feeling when you are lighter, when the clothes just fit and don\u2019t make any effort to contain the body within. Overall it requires one to change the entire routine one follows and be absolutely religious about it. I would confess I didn\u2019t adhere 100% to what I had planned but 100% was always the goal, so don\u2019t feel disheartened if you fail at some time, but make sure you learn from it. The goal should be 100% effort always. Health is a lifelong habit, so make amendments to your life such that they stick with your forever. Get in the habit of reading good articles and research related to wellbeing. Learn yoga and meditation, they will be your guiding light for the days to come.\\n\\nCouple of videos that helped me visualize what I wanted to achieve.  \\n[http://embed.ted.com/talks/lang/en/sandra\\\\_aamodt\\\\_why\\\\_dieting\\\\_doesn\\\\_t\\\\_usually\\\\_work.html](http://embed.ted.com/talks/lang/en/sandra_aamodt_why_dieting_doesn_t_usually_work.html)\\n\\n[http://embed.ted.com/talks/lang/en/matt\\\\_cutts\\\\_try\\\\_something\\\\_new\\\\_for\\\\_30\\\\_days.html](http://embed.ted.com/talks/lang/en/matt_cutts_try_something_new_for_30_days.html)"},{"id":"/2014/03/19/atheism-2-0","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2014/03/19/atheism-2-0","source":"@site/blog/2014-03-19-atheism-2-0/index.md","title":"Atheism 2.0","description":"I always preferred being called an agnostic. Although I don\u2019t buy any philosophy that religions profess, I quite didn\u2019t appreciate being called an atheist. Atheism somehow meant vanity for me. There is so much that we do not know about our own nature and the nature of the universe that surrounds us. We are deceived\u2026","date":"2014-03-19T17:50:43.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Education","permalink":"/blog/tags/education"},{"inline":true,"label":"Atheism","permalink":"/blog/tags/atheism"},{"inline":true,"label":"featured","permalink":"/blog/tags/featured"},{"inline":true,"label":"Religion","permalink":"/blog/tags/religion"}],"readingTime":3.22,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Ajay More","title":"Maintainer of ajaymore.in","url":"https://github.com/ajaymore","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7659804?v=4","key":"ajay","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Atheism 2.0","date":"2014-03-19T17:50:43.000Z","authors":["ajay"],"tags":["Education","Atheism","featured","Religion"],"description":"I always preferred being called an agnostic. Although I don\u2019t buy any philosophy that religions profess, I quite didn\u2019t appreciate being called an atheist. Atheism somehow meant vanity for me. There is so much that we do not know about our own nature and the nature of the universe that surrounds us. We are deceived\u2026","image":"./670pxatheism_symbol-svg_.jpg","mdx":{"format":"md"}},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"10 pounds lighter","permalink":"/blog/2014/03/21/10-pounds-lighter"},"nextItem":{"title":"Mangalore City","permalink":"/blog/2014/02/13/mangalore-city"}},"content":"I always preferred being called an agnostic. Although I don\u2019t buy any philosophy that religions profess, I quite didn\u2019t appreciate being called an atheist. Atheism somehow meant vanity for me. There is so much that we do not know about our own nature and the nature of the universe that surrounds us. We are deceived by our senses and brain perceptions at almost every stage of life and yet how can we be absolutely certain about our faith. It was this dilemma that kept me from calling myself an atheist. I was open to all the convincing arguments put forward by religions so that I could switch sides with them, but somehow luckily for me that never happened. As I learnt more about religion, the more I distanced myself from it.\xa0\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nAlmost all religions began with exceptional individuals who had extraordinary experiences in their lives. Be it Buddha, Moses, Jesus, Mohammed or saints and sages of the ancient India and abroad. But if we are to consider current research in neuroscience these supposed godly experiences of all these extraordinary men would be simulated in a laboratory in a near foreseeable future. All god experiences are nothing more than psychosomatic experiences and there is enough science literature to back it. If we are to go by statistics alone 99.9% of world population has never had a god experience, but still finds it convenient to believe in one. So yes, if you take pride in belonging to 21st century please don\u2019t buy the hogwash that the religions have to preach.\\n\\nBut there are quite a many wonderful things that I have learnt being associated with religious people. My love for these ways of living makes me a kind of religious man, yet who does not really believe in religion, quite paradoxical \u{1F642} . So I preferred being an agnostic until now. Although I believe the world will be a better place to live without religion. Although I am sure the general hatred among the masses will exponentially reduce with the death of religion. And although we will have enough time to focus on the ideals of life and principles of morality when religion is removed from our conscience, I somehow cannot guarantee myself the fruitfulness of the endeavor. There are so many things that we would miss if there wasn\u2019t a religion.\\n\\nOkay! So if I were to just brush aside the idea of religion, there would be so much I will be missing in life. The art of meditation or mindful awareness that I have come to appreciate, where does it stand in absence of religion? The religious congregations where I get to meet so many people whom I cannot meet otherwise, and where the people meet only for the sake of it and not for business, what happens to these communal events in the absence of religion? The grand temples, churches, mosques and other beautiful architectural wonders that are symbols of religions, what significance do they hold in absence of the reason for which they were erected? A filler, a companion in difficult times, what do we have at such times if not god? And so many other aspects of our social and personal life, that are based on religious beliefs and are quite beneficial to our well-being, what would possibly replace them if we cast aside religion. Definitely Atheism can\u2019t replace all of it. If we are to go by current understanding of atheism by the general public, it\u2019s plain boring.\\n\\n> We need to revive atheism. We need to make people realize how rewarding it can be, to be secular. And to the rescue comes the man Alain de Botton. He talks about Atheism 2.0 in the below talk and produces a convincing argument.\\n\\n[http://embed.ted.com/talks/lang/en/alain\\\\_de\\\\_botton\\\\_atheism\\\\_2\\\\_0.html](http://embed.ted.com/talks/lang/en/alain_de_botton_atheism_2_0.html)"},{"id":"/2014/02/13/mangalore-city","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2014/02/13/mangalore-city","source":"@site/blog/2014-02-13-mangalore-city/index.md","title":"Mangalore City","description":"Sitting in the train door, legs on the stairs, cool night air thrashing against our face. Yes that was the beginning of a wonderful and memorable trip to Mangalore. The berth next to a rickety door in the 2nd AC compartment, and the constant creaking noise of the door opening and closing couldn\u2019t really dampen\u2026","date":"2014-02-13T16:51:15.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Trek & Travel","permalink":"/blog/tags/trek-travel"},{"inline":true,"label":"adventure","permalink":"/blog/tags/adventure"},{"inline":true,"label":"beach","permalink":"/blog/tags/beach"},{"inline":true,"label":"food","permalink":"/blog/tags/food"},{"inline":true,"label":"travel","permalink":"/blog/tags/travel"}],"readingTime":8.22,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Ajay More","title":"Maintainer of ajaymore.in","url":"https://github.com/ajaymore","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7659804?v=4","key":"ajay","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Mangalore City","date":"2014-02-13T16:51:15.000Z","authors":["ajay"],"tags":["Trek & Travel","adventure","beach","food","travel"],"description":"Sitting in the train door, legs on the stairs, cool night air thrashing against our face. Yes that was the beginning of a wonderful and memorable trip to Mangalore. The berth next to a rickety door in the 2nd AC compartment, and the constant creaking noise of the door opening and closing couldn\u2019t really dampen\u2026","image":"./img_20131208_121119176_1.jpg","mdx":{"format":"md"}},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Atheism 2.0","permalink":"/blog/2014/03/19/atheism-2-0"},"nextItem":{"title":"42kms\u2026","permalink":"/blog/2014/01/27/42kms"}},"content":"Sitting in the train door, legs on the stairs, cool night air thrashing against our face. Yes that was the beginning of a wonderful and memorable trip to [Mangalore](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mangalore). The berth next to a rickety door in the 2nd AC compartment, and the constant creaking noise of the door opening and closing couldn\u2019t really dampen my spirits. Me and my friend Abhinav reached the station early morning next day for a pleasant surprise called the city of Mangalore.\xa0\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nOur friend Shrinivas was already at the station with his overworked yamaha FZ two wheeler. Seriously the bike demanded a little attention and so did the helmet that he carried. We did a Tripsy (3 people on a two wheeler) from the station to a nearby place where we could catch an auto rikshaw. The auto wala dropped us at Kottara, the place where our beloved friend stays. The place is also home to the Infosys campus in Mangalore city.\\n\\nIt seemed like Shrini had taken quite a time planning this outing of ours. Like a seasoned travel guide he got us ready in no time, and we were down for breakfast. We had a nice Dosa for breakfast and as soon as we were finished, we headed towards a nearby beach. To reach the beach we had to cross a river by ferry and then behold the beautiful beach called Tannirbhavi. Its a wonderful feeling when your mind is empty and cannot go into it\u2019s automatic day dreaming mode. In a new location it constantly keeps gazing, capturing and recapturing every frame, trying to make sense of the new environment. If you happen to be with nature at such times, the feeling is simply of awe and wonderment. We clicked few pictures along the way and really appreciated our time at the sunny beach. Once we were back to the river in order to get back to the city, I couldn\u2019t help and wonder at the guy who was down in the water at the river bank. The people there were trying to build some kind of jetty to create a stopping platform for incoming boats. So the guys on the bank carried sand bags and dumped them in the river. And this guy in the water would dive in, get hold of the dumped sand bag at the river bed and secure it in place. And this continued. I noticed that the work was in progress when we first got down from the ferry, before moving to the beach. And now we were back almost an hour later and still this guy was down in the water working. Wonder where his super strength came from.\\n\\nNow it was time for some delicious Mangalore fish at Giri Manjas. Shrini had in his itinerary the best place to give us an authentic sea food experience. That seemingly unnoticeable place had its name appear in the National Geographic\u2019s traveller magazine. True to its reputation the food was spicy and delicious. We had King Fish, pomfret, prawns and some other delicacies. Then we moved to another beautiful location where we had sumptuous ice-cream that just quenched all my appetite. All the food we had so far had a quality about it and yet was way cheaper. Compare it with restaurant rates in Pune where I come from, I just felt rich.\\n\\nThe afternoon wasn\u2019t yet over. We moved to the mall called City Center and watched the movie \u201CFrozen\u201D, the movie is mesmerizing, do watch it if you haven\u2019t done already. Poor Shrini though had forgotten his precious glasses and had to see the 3D movie in a whole new dimension, no wonder why he didn\u2019t like it. We strolled around in the mall for some time and then headed back for some beach time. The city is quite small by comparison, so you can reach almost any location from anywhere in less than 15 minutes and as less as 7 rupees. The transport system is efficient and you wouldn\u2019t be frustrated if you decide upon using the public transport alone. I was quite skeptical at first about the privately run transport buses. The buses had no pattern about them which is characteristic of an organized city transport. Windows from most buses were just open metallic frames with no glass. However poor those buses looked, I had absolutely no complaints about the job they did. I was just flying around the city within no time. Mangalore transport system is quite efficient.\\n\\nFrom back home now we headed towards a new beach called Panambur. We had the best of our times here. The beach is clean, the sand is beautiful and the sun was setting. A perfect combination for an ultimate beach experience. Abhinav was so impressed he expressed his appreciation something like this; \u201CThis is the bested, cleanest, warmest, greatest, awesomest, sexiest beach.\u201D We played a good amount of time in water. And Shrini was again back at our heels marshalling us back home for the next adventure. We were back, had a bath and were ready for some party.\\n\\n![Panambur](./img_20131207_181856_1.jpg)\\n\\nShrini took us to an awesome pub called The Liquid lounge. The place is famous for its loud rock music, delicious food and an awesome crowd. Few pegs down and we were on a real high. The loud music was becoming more bearable after few drinks \u{1F61B} . We went out of our way to go back to the DJ and get him play our favourite songs. This is the place where you can definitely practice your rock singing (shouting) skills. By the time we were done, none of us were in any good shape to do anything seemingly logical. The poor auto rickshaw guy had to repeatedly listen to Abhinav\u2019s newly received knowledge of great Kannada language. Back to the apartment, nothing could keep my eyes open any longer, I just **slept.**\\n\\nWe woke up early next morning and yes the Delhi election results were being declared, sure the result for 3 other states were also being declared but let\u2019s call it Delhi election results for simplicity. The drama surrounding the election results added to our day\u2019s enjoyment. We started our journey to Udupi in the morning. The plan was to visit Malape beach and St Mary\u2019s Island. We reached Malape beach and took a boat ride to the St. Mary\u2019s Island. A ferry was available but it\u2019s an entirely different experience to travel by boat on an open sea. The Island is straight out of a movie. Although island doesn\u2019t have a sand beach, It has lots of beautiful rocky pools to swim and take amazing pictures. It also has a shell beach. Here you can click anywhere and you have an awesome wallpaper photo in your camera. I don\u2019t have the literary skills required to describe something that beautiful, just see the picture below and make a judgement for yourself. While returning back me and Abhinav got a rare opportunity to seat atop the bow (front part of the boat). It was as good as sitting on the water itself, just a few inches from it, the experience was simply exhilarating. Throughout the day we constantly tapped our smart phones to check the election result update, it was just way to thrilling to miss out. Next we got down at Manipal University on our way back. Strolled around in the famous university campus and returned back to Mangalore.\\n\\n![St. Mary\'s](./mangalore_1.jpg)\\n\\nWe had a train to catch at 8:30 pm in the night. So we quickly got back and got ready to have dinner. Shrini wasn\u2019t yet finished with his plan. He had one more surprise in store for us; \u201CSea Crabs\u201D. He took us to a wonderful restaurant called Village. We just had enough time to quickly order, enjoy the food and catch the train. After enjoying some beer we got lost in a wonderful discussion. The discussion revolved around doing something besides our regular jobs. On the lines of startups. Doing something different, chasing new ideas. The interesting discussion lasted a very very long time. The first time somebody saw the watch we were already missing our train. We called the station and the train was running on time and we were definitely going to miss it as our crab wasn\u2019t ordered yet \u{1F642} . There was this mixed feeling, that crops up when something does not turn out as per the plan. Me and Abhinav both had office to reach the next day. But the enjoyment of past many hours was so astounding that we were somewhat happy that we missed the train and had more time with us now. The crab was good and we took our own time to finish.\\n\\nMoved about in the city on Shrini\u2019s bike in the night. Hope he gives it the utmost necessary servicing it requires soon enough. The next morning had an awesome breakfast at an awesome restaurant called \u201CChutney\u201D. Had our tickets booked for our return journey by a volvo bus and then bade goodbye to Mangalore just before noon.\\n\\nI loved the city and would like to visit it again. Few things worth mentioning; I didn\u2019t find any bill boards in the city with ugly politicians on them. Mangaloreans do have a sense of aesthetics. I was quite surprised when I observed nobody supervising the theatre exit door when people returned the 3D glasses received before watching the movie. There is no visible language bias in the city and national language is respected well here. If you love travelling please put this city on your list.\\n\\nAll thanks to Shrinivas for such a wonderful journey. He has set the bar high when it comes to being a host. We will make sure you have same amount of fun when you come visit our city."},{"id":"/2014/01/27/42kms","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2014/01/27/42kms","source":"@site/blog/2014-01-27-42kms/index.md","title":"42kms\u2026","description":"I am quite comfortable running a half marathon, read my post first half marathon on how I got started. So the next adventure in line was off course completing the full marathon. When I made it public that I was participating in a full marathon, it was wonderful to observe peoples reaction. Some believed in\u2026","date":"2014-01-27T06:29:15.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Physical Performance","permalink":"/blog/tags/physical-performance"},{"inline":true,"label":"endurance","permalink":"/blog/tags/endurance"},{"inline":true,"label":"featured","permalink":"/blog/tags/featured"},{"inline":true,"label":"Marathon","permalink":"/blog/tags/marathon"}],"readingTime":5.69,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Ajay More","title":"Maintainer of ajaymore.in","url":"https://github.com/ajaymore","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7659804?v=4","key":"ajay","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"42kms\u2026","date":"2014-01-27T06:29:15.000Z","authors":["ajay"],"tags":["Physical Performance","endurance","featured","Marathon"],"description":"I am quite comfortable running a half marathon, read my post first half marathon on how I got started. So the next adventure in line was off course completing the full marathon. When I made it public that I was participating in a full marathon, it was wonderful to observe peoples reaction. Some believed in\u2026","image":"./run.png","mdx":{"format":"md"}},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Mangalore City","permalink":"/blog/2014/02/13/mangalore-city"},"nextItem":{"title":"Free Software","permalink":"/blog/2013/12/18/free-software"}},"content":"I am quite comfortable running a half marathon, read my post [first half marathon](http://vagabondme.com/first-half-marathon/) on how I got started. So the next adventure in line was off course completing the full marathon. When I made it public that I was participating in a full marathon, it was wonderful to observe peoples reaction. Some believed in me, encouraged me saying I could do it. Some thought I was joking and I would run only a half marathon if at all I did. And my good friends just noticed me being more crazy than my usual.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nTo be honest I like running, but I am not a big fan of long arduous practice sessions. More than 10-15 rounds on a 400mtr track almost makes me giddy and is painfully boring. Even if I ran a new route in the city I would run a maximum of 6-7 kilometers. I also tried my best to be consistent, but I failed there too. Critical office project at the time meant more time spent in office and returning home totally exhausted. It was really difficult at times to harness enough will power to get myself wearing those pretty running shoes and start jogging. Whenever I did I knew it wasn\u2019t hard as it seemed, but the beginning is always difficult. Until 3 weeks prior to the marathon, I wasn\u2019t really in best shape to complete a marathon.\\n\\nI knew I won\u2019t be able to cover a lot of ground within 3 weeks although my work load had considerably gone down and I could really focus on my training. However I wanted to make sure that I did cross that finish line no matter what. One thing I had learnt from my previous experiences in long distance running and endurance trekking was that; you should always take a break whenever your body starts to give a signal that it\u2019s breaking down. If you keep persistent by being foolishly confident of your endurance and will power, the only thing that will happen is, you being exposed to an injury which will most likely stop you from doing anything more. The old wisdom \u201Cslow and steady wins the race\u201D does come handy here. So my plan was quite simple, to keep my body in as much optimal state as possible. My practice did not involve any long distance running at all. I had hardly run 5-6kms at a stretch during last 3 weeks of practice. Instead I focused on other areas too, I walked long distances after dinner like 2-3kms. Then I did a good deal of yoga in the morning. Also I completed a strenuous trek one weekend, staying in the sun the entire day, to test how much stress my body could take in.\\n\\nPrior to the marathon, I happened to get a lot of interesting tips & tricks from an old school friend. He advised me to keep taking fluids throughout the run and always keep a water bottle ready in hand. To pour water at the back of my neck, when it felt hot, so that the coolness will spread to my head and keep my nerve steady. And some more genuine tips which I needn\u2019t specify here. For me the marathon was going to be a tandem of walk and run until I reached the finish line. Although more prepared than ever, 42kms stood like the Mount Everest. I was still unsure if I could reach in one piece to the finish line in less than 6 hours. And like always even during this marathon\u2019s previous night we had hardly 4 hours of sleep.\\n\\nThe run began at 6:40 am. The sun wasn\u2019t up yet, and the weather was cooler. Running along the seaside felt like dream. Since the distance was long, I wasn\u2019t really focused on the distance markers. I hardly looked at any of them. I didn\u2019t put on my iPod as listening to it could have given me a sense of time. It was quite weird, I didn\u2019t want to know the time. I wanted it to pass unnoticed when I kept persistent at my job. I enjoyed running like never before, not trying to hurry at all, not getting myself out of breath, but just smiling and running that\u2019s all.\\n\\nThe enthusiastic Mumbai crowd was by the side on the streets cheering us. The small children raised their hands for HiFi and would feel very happy when the runner touched their hand. I could imagine myself in their place, I could imagine how wonderful I would have felt when a marathoner gave me a HiFi. I was in no hurry, soaking in the atmosphere, listening to every cheer, smiling and enjoying my run continued. I took my first break after reaching the half marathon marker. Stopped for a while, used some ice, stretched a little and then carried on.\\n\\nNow the sun was making its presence felt. My speed was dropping and I required more walking breaks than before. The enthusiasm had not dropped one little bit, but body glucose level kept dropping and I could sense it. At 35kms I started to get little cramps in my calves while running. I could walk fine, and knew for certain that if I walked the rest of the distance I would easily complete the race in less than 6 hours. A volunteer along the way had offered me a small relief spray. I made a judicious use of the spray. Under no condition I wanted to expose myself to an injury which will cut short my progress in the run. I used plenty of ice. I would put small pieces in my shoes so that the foot muscles felt relaxed. Those muscles were in real pain and wouldn\u2019t soothe by relief spray as it didn\u2019t work on socks. The last 10 kms were long and hard but were in no way difficult.\\n\\nSlowly and steadily I was nearing the finish line. I realized I could finish the race in under 5 hours, so I picked up the pace along last 2kms. The cramps however kept me from making any considerable progress. When it was about 600mtr before finish, I stopped. I wanted to have a photogenic finish \u{1F61B} . Didn\u2019t want to cross the line with the obvious pain and limp. I used the remaining of relief spray to calm the muscles. Walked up 200 more metres and then finally when I could see the distant finish line and the cheering crowd I picked up my space. I ran as hard as I could, ran as if I just started the race, and was there in a flash. Could see the clock now that I saw at the start of the race. The time was back on my mind."},{"id":"/2013/12/18/free-software","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2013/12/18/free-software","source":"@site/blog/2013-12-18-free-software/index.md","title":"Free Software","description":"The Wikipedia article on VLC media player says: \u201CVLC is first in the sourceforge.net overall download count and has been downloaded more than 1.3 billion times\u201D. It works on all major operating systems Windows, OS X, Linux, BSD, Solaris, Android, iOS and few more. VLC has around 600,000 Lines of code, VLC uses a lot\u2026","date":"2013-12-18T09:09:15.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Information Tech","permalink":"/blog/tags/information-tech"},{"inline":true,"label":"Open-source","permalink":"/blog/tags/open-source"}],"readingTime":4.65,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Ajay More","title":"Maintainer of ajaymore.in","url":"https://github.com/ajaymore","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7659804?v=4","key":"ajay","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Free Software","date":"2013-12-18T09:09:15.000Z","authors":["ajay"],"tags":["Information Tech","Open-source"],"description":"The Wikipedia article on VLC media player says: \u201CVLC is first in the sourceforge.net overall download count and has been downloaded more than 1.3 billion times\u201D. It works on all major operating systems Windows, OS X, Linux, BSD, Solaris, Android, iOS and few more. VLC has around 600,000 Lines of code, VLC uses a lot\u2026","image":"./copy-left.png","mdx":{"format":"md"}},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"42kms\u2026","permalink":"/blog/2014/01/27/42kms"},"nextItem":{"title":"If \u2014 My Inspiration","permalink":"/blog/2013/11/15/if-inspirational-poem"}},"content":"The Wikipedia article on VLC media player says: \u201CVLC is first in the sourceforge.net overall download count and has been downloaded more than 1.3 billion times\u201D. It works on all major operating systems Windows, OS X, Linux, BSD, Solaris, Android, iOS and few more. VLC has around 600,000 Lines of code, VLC uses a lot of external libraries, which are over 3,020,000 lines of code. This is all handwritten code, work of a decade long programming cycle. The software is available in 48 languages. And needless to say integral part of our virtual lives.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nSo now coming back to the title of the post, yes VLC media player is a free, open-source software. People can download and use it the way they want without any restriction. If you are programming types, you can download its source code, modify it, change whatever you want, turn traffic cone into traffic signal and redistribute, no questions would be asked. Most people cannot understand how incredible it is, but I will try to throw some light. Firstly we are really not grateful enough to the open-source community out of shear ignorance of what they do to make our lives better. We do not realize how easier and cheaper our life has become thanks to the accumulated works of these countless programmers around the world.\\n\\nEvery computing device we use today, proprietary or otherwise, either uses open source software or is in some way inspired (copied) from it. A report by the Standish Group (from 2008) states that adoption of open-source software models has resulted in savings of about $60 billion per year to consumers. So how do these software come into being, when nobody is paying the developers who develop it? What inspires these individuals around the world to put in the mind boggling effort that is required to write efficient code? What moral code dictates them to distribute the code just like that and not expect a penny for it?\\n\\nAnswer appears from different directions, for some writing code is a form of art, a sort of poetry that should be shared with one and all. For others it\u2019s a community service, their own way of giving back to the world, remember the $60 billion saving to the consumer. For few its a way of bringing transparency into the world of software industry where companies charge fortunes to their client for nothing. To some it\u2019s a way of creating more secure systems that are tried and tested by everyone around the world and not behind the closed doors of a corporation. Open-source development is a way of getting connected with geniuses around the world and learning from their work. Its a unified world with no boundaries. its a way of life with great sense of work ethic & passion without any sort of discrimination.\\n\\nThe open source community takes Isaac Newton\u2019s words: \u201CI stand on the shoulders of the giants\u201D literally. The code evolves every single day based on the great products built in the past. Every day new libraries are created and a few days later a library that manages these libraries is created. I would not dwell into details of which free software affects you how, that would be overwhelming. The evolving field of software development is driven by community and not some corporate ingenuity. Most solutions to great problems are found on the internet in public forums, it\u2019s more of a collaborative effort. Big companies realise this and they are also making huge contributions to the open-source movement, either with man power or with money.\\n\\nI really adore the work of people like Richard stallman, Eric.S.Raymond, Bruce Perens and many more who have pioneered the idea of free software. It took some time for industry imbibe this idea of how willingness to share ones work with others will help them manifold, as other\u2019s too will make similar contributions. People go through each other\u2019s code; criticise it, find mistakes with it, most times correct it themselves. Open source development is a vibrant world, where you get to learn on an exponential scale through the mistakes and achievements of others.\\n\\nThis article isn\u2019t intended to draw sympathy towards the work of these dedicated folks. It\u2019s just a reminder to all that, you really haven\u2019t paid for everything that you enjoy today. Please use these words consciously. Like free software developers, countless others in countless other disciplines strive every day selflessly to make this world a better place to live, without expecting much in return. Next time you don\u2019t like a free app on android store please don\u2019t just write \u201CThis app sucks!!\u201D in comments. If you don\u2019t like just uninstall it. If at all you want to vent out your frustration be more constructive and write a critical review of the app and explain to the developer why the app sucks. Rate the applications on android that you like, least you can do. If you use torrent seed your torrents, don\u2019t be stingy.\\n\\nThere is no end to where software industry will take us. Human imagination is the limit. Some day with improved computation, continued digitization of historical records, archival of internet blogs & new articles, deep study of social network will lead to an artificial intelligence that can almost mimic an average human mind. We have already heavily dependent on computers for our physical and mental wellbeing, so why not get involved with it. If you cannot learn to code teach your children, it\u2019s not really that difficult and can become a wonderful hobby for a lifetime."},{"id":"/2013/11/15/if-inspirational-poem","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2013/11/15/if-inspirational-poem","source":"@site/blog/2013-11-15-if-inspirational-poem/index.md","title":"If \u2014 My Inspiration","description":"If you can keep your head when all about you Are losing theirs and blaming it on you, If you can trust yourself when all men doubt you, But make allowance for their doubting too; If you can wait and not be tired by waiting, Or being lied about, don\u2019t deal in lies, Or being\u2026","date":"2013-11-15T07:27:36.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Leisure","permalink":"/blog/tags/leisure"},{"inline":true,"label":"inspiration","permalink":"/blog/tags/inspiration"},{"inline":true,"label":"poem","permalink":"/blog/tags/poem"}],"readingTime":1.47,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Ajay More","title":"Maintainer of ajaymore.in","url":"https://github.com/ajaymore","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7659804?v=4","key":"ajay","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"If \u2014 My Inspiration","date":"2013-11-15T07:27:36.000Z","authors":["ajay"],"tags":["Leisure","inspiration","poem"],"description":"If you can keep your head when all about you Are losing theirs and blaming it on you, If you can trust yourself when all men doubt you, But make allowance for their doubting too; If you can wait and not be tired by waiting, Or being lied about, don\u2019t deal in lies, Or being\u2026","image":"./rudyard-kipling-008.png","mdx":{"format":"md"}},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Free Software","permalink":"/blog/2013/12/18/free-software"},"nextItem":{"title":"The Lost Wallet","permalink":"/blog/2013/10/30/lost-wallet"}},"content":"If you can keep your head when all about you  \\nAre losing theirs and blaming it on you,  \\nIf you can trust yourself when all men doubt you,  \\nBut make allowance for their doubting too;\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nIf you can wait and not be tired by waiting,  \\nOr being lied about, don\u2019t deal in lies,  \\nOr being hated, don\u2019t give way to hating,  \\nAnd yet don\u2019t look too good, nor talk too wise:\\n\\nIf you can dream \u2013 and not make dreams your master;  \\nIf you can think \u2013 and not make thoughts your aim;  \\nIf you can meet with Triumph and Disaster  \\nAnd treat those two impostors just the same;\\n\\nIf you can bear to hear the truth you\u2019ve spoken  \\nTwisted by knaves to make a trap for fools,  \\nOr watch the things you gave your life to broken,  \\nAnd stoop and build \u2019em up with wornout tools:\\n\\nIf you can make one heap of all your winnings  \\nAnd risk it on one turn of pitch-and-toss,  \\nAnd lose, and start again at your beginnings  \\nAnd never breathe a word about your loss;\\n\\nIf you can force your heart and nerve and sinew  \\nTo serve your turn long after they are gone,  \\nAnd so hold on when there is nothing in you  \\nExcept the Will which says to them: \u2018Hold on!\u2019\\n\\nIf you can talk with crowds and keep your virtue,  \\nOr walk with kings \u2013 nor lose the common touch,  \\nIf neither foes nor loving friends can hurt you,  \\nIf all men count with you, but none too much;\\n\\nIf you can fill the unforgiving minute  \\nWith sixty seconds\u2019 worth of distance run \u2013  \\nYours is the Earth and everything that\u2019s in it,  \\nAnd \u2013 which is more \u2013 you\u2019ll be a Man my son!\\n\\n**Rudyard Kipling.**"},{"id":"/2013/10/30/lost-wallet","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2013/10/30/lost-wallet","source":"@site/blog/2013-10-30-lost-wallet/index.md","title":"The Lost Wallet","description":"Of many dreadful experiences in life, one is of losing your wallet. Losing money is the least of our headaches at such times. The process of getting a brand new Credit/Debit card or a new driving license is almost killing. The thing is, there is not enough care in the world that can ensure your\u2026","date":"2013-10-30T08:38:01.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Leisure","permalink":"/blog/tags/leisure"},{"inline":true,"label":"featured","permalink":"/blog/tags/featured"},{"inline":true,"label":"Morality","permalink":"/blog/tags/morality"}],"readingTime":6.34,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Ajay More","title":"Maintainer of ajaymore.in","url":"https://github.com/ajaymore","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7659804?v=4","key":"ajay","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"The Lost Wallet","date":"2013-10-30T08:38:01.000Z","authors":["ajay"],"tags":["Leisure","featured","Morality"],"description":"Of many dreadful experiences in life, one is of losing your wallet. Losing money is the least of our headaches at such times. The process of getting a brand new Credit/Debit card or a new driving license is almost killing. The thing is, there is not enough care in the world that can ensure your\u2026","image":"./wallet.jpg","mdx":{"format":"md"}},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"If \u2014 My Inspiration","permalink":"/blog/2013/11/15/if-inspirational-poem"},"nextItem":{"title":"Grit","permalink":"/blog/2013/10/03/grit"}},"content":"Of many dreadful experiences in life, one is of losing your wallet. Losing money is the least of our headaches at such times. The process of getting a brand new Credit/Debit card or a new driving license is almost killing. The thing is, there is not enough care in the world that can ensure your wallet doesn\u2019t get lost. People lose things one way or another, it just happens. There are successful organized gangs that do pick pocketing and are thriving. So yes if luck favors we are quite likely to get pick pocketed.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nOnce it happened, I was visiting a friend. My wallet was in my back pocket as I rode my bike. There were a few traffic signals on the way, and were quite jam packed. Once I reached my destination, got my bike parked and got ready to move to a small eatery, I involuntarily reached my pocket to check the wallet. And my heart just skipped a bit. There was this sudden sunken filling as if I just lost something irreplaceable. I checked my pocket again knowing well the futility of action. The heart beat was picking up speed like a bolting athlete. And for a moment I was just dazed, lost and empty.\\n\\nMy friend arrived, and a sudden smile appeared on my face out of nowhere. Friends mean so much! I told him what had happened. Every ordeal is a joke when discussed with your buddy and this was no different. At first he was like, I knew it all along, with your careless attitude this was going to happen someday. Any ways tell me what all things you lost. And we started analysing the gravity of the situation. Once the situation was read, first task was to block the ATM cards. We went to the nearest internet cafe, got customer care numbers of the two banks which trusted me with those beautiful plastic cards. I dialed in and requested them to block my cards with immediate effect.\\n\\nNext we went to a nearby police station to report the matter. We thought far ahead, maybe if my wallet was picked by a criminal who got caught at any point. Then it will be more trouble for me as all sorts of questions like what is your driving license doing with this person? How does this man hold your credit card? Would crop up. If you say it was lost then a question would be asked, where is the police complaint? So we thought it was wise to just lodge a FIR as a proof that my wallet was really lost. I suspected that it was stolen at some damn traffic signal. But when I narrated my story to the police, they came up with standard excuses like not under our jurisdiction etc. to just whisk us away. I didn\u2019t have enough will left in me to fight them anymore, so I just gave up. Although it feels me with remorse as to why I didn\u2019t stand up, it felt most wise at that time to just give it up.\\n\\nOnce such a thing happens, it\u2019s a huge pain to explain to the rest of the world what really happened. You have to repeat the same story a hundred times. As most of us have this flair for spicy conversations we always tend to extrapolate things. But for me huge effort was to remember if I had extrapolated something so that my account of the story wasn\u2019t in conflict with what I had told to someone else. People provide different opinions. Some will provide you with wisdom like, it\u2019s never a good idea to keep your wallet in your back pocket while travelling, or when you are in the crowd. It should always be your front pocket at such times. Some people will say, it\u2019s never correct to put all your money at one place like your wallet, instead it should be spread all over your pockets. Hell! How cumbersome that is, I would prefer losing my wallet again. And then there are parents who will look at you like a policeman looks at a criminal and make you believe that it was all your fault. It was quite funny to observe.\\n\\nSo the news was finally out in the public. Next I had to go to the bank to get some cash out. I was back in ancient times, filling out bank slips. I attempted that forgotten signature again, man I never get it correct the first time. Stood in the long queue, while collecting cash the cashier observed I had not updated my bank book in 2 years and coyly asked me if I had lost my ATM card? A little amused I asked how she guessed? She replied people visit the bank for such small withdrawals only when they can\u2019t use their ATMs. Then I filled up the form for a new ATM and got out. Next went to another bank some kilometers away followed the same dull process and the day was up. When you have a bank work, typically that\u2019s the only work you do in the entire day. Next big headache was getting my driving license back. That was going to be a real pain with FIR and all required. So I decided to just postpone the activity for time being.\\n\\nJust 3 days later when I returned home from my office I saw an unusual package lying on the table. The envelope of the package was made out of notebook paper. A few pages from an old notebook must have been torn to make it. The package was sent by speed post and had the postage seal on it. I could make out the place from where the package was sent by looking at the postal seal. It was a place nearby to where I parked my bike on the day I lost my wallet. On the envelope there was my name and address handwritten in big letters. I quickly opened the package and the sight just blew my mind. There emerged my beautiful wallet in perfect condition. I have not enough words to express my amazement and joy. In the envelope there was also a note, which I opened and began reading.\\n\\nThe note read. Dear Mr. Ajay, you dropped your wallet at a traffic signal last night. I picked it up and searched through it to see if I could find your number. When I couldn\u2019t find it, I used the details on your driving license to find you on Facebook. However I could not find you on Facebook. So finally I decided to post it back to you on the mentioned address. Please make sure you take good care of your precious belongings next time. I read the letter and reread it few more times. It meant a lot. There wasn\u2019t a mention of the sender anywhere on or inside the package. It doesn\u2019t really matter, I will remember the person for the rest of my life.\\n\\nThe package left me with lots of lessons to be followed over a lifetime. My own happiness and joy was a proof enough to do something good in life and not desire to see its impact directly. If you are doing good it will always bring a smile to someone somewhere, you don\u2019t really need to see it. The world is not really bad as we perceive it, it\u2019s just that our eyes are not wide open to see those beautiful people with their wonderful hearts making a difference day in day out."},{"id":"/2013/10/03/grit","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2013/10/03/grit","source":"@site/blog/2013-10-03-grit/index.md","title":"Grit","description":"Grit is passion and perseverance for very long term goals. Grit is having stamina. Grit is sticking with your future, day in, day out, not just for the week, not just for the month, but for years and working very hard to make that future a reality. Grit is living life like it\u2019s a marathon,\u2026","date":"2013-10-03T17:58:04.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"self development","permalink":"/blog/tags/self-development"},{"inline":true,"label":"Grit","permalink":"/blog/tags/grit"}],"readingTime":2.6,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Ajay More","title":"Maintainer of ajaymore.in","url":"https://github.com/ajaymore","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7659804?v=4","key":"ajay","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Grit","date":"2013-10-03T17:58:04.000Z","authors":["ajay"],"tags":["self development","Grit"],"description":"Grit is passion and perseverance for very long term goals. Grit is having stamina. Grit is sticking with your future, day in, day out, not just for the week, not just for the month, but for years and working very hard to make that future a reality. Grit is living life like it\u2019s a marathon,\u2026","image":"./true_grit_619x357-slideshow.png","mdx":{"format":"md"}},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"The Lost Wallet","permalink":"/blog/2013/10/30/lost-wallet"},"nextItem":{"title":"Sing, Smile and do Service","permalink":"/blog/2013/09/26/sing-smile-and-service"}},"content":"> Grit is passion and perseverance for very long term goals. Grit is having stamina. Grit is sticking with your future, day in, day out, not just for the week, not just for the month, but for years and working very hard to make that future a reality. Grit is living life like it\u2019s a marathon, not a sprint!  \\n> **Excerpt from the talk on TED by Angela Duckworth.**\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n[https://embed.ted.com/talks/angela\\\\_lee\\\\_duckworth\\\\_the\\\\_key\\\\_to\\\\_success\\\\_grit.html](https://embed.ted.com/talks/angela_lee_duckworth_the_key_to_success_grit.html)\\n\\nThere is a lot of research happening currently around the psychological trait \u2018Grit\u2019. Most people will claim that they know what grit means but it\u2019s not an easy task to develop it. Actually I feel the other way, it\u2019s the ignorance of our own nature that stops us from becoming grittier. If we look back at our lives, there are some things we are good at today because we have worked on them for very long, maybe months, years or perhaps decades. A small child learning to walk, climb, talk is the best example of grit. Those skills aren\u2019t just mastered suddenly, the muscles develop over time and the child keeps trying. The way children learn how to read and write is again an example of grit. Learning alphabets is just too much work for a small child, but as the time goes, with repeated application and persistent effort the child becomes better at it and if the effort continues forever she might become a good prose writer someday. The same goes for any other skill.\\n\\nSo the bottom line is this. If we really wish to develop a new skill, we should not quit let happen what. We might fail, we might give up for some time, but come what may we should come back and start over again. Just give that skill enough time to flourish. Think of it as an insurance policy that would mature in a long foreseeable future. If a good health is your goal, make it a habit to workout at least 5min every day. Whenever you feel more motivated put in more effort, but 5min. should be your everyday target no matter what. Trust me it will improve your physique like it never did. Grit is not the perfection of a discipline but a process of building up a discipline with sincere effort. We would fail like we always do, we might lose touch for some time, we might give up but if we really want to achieve we will come back to it again and again and never stop.\\n\\nThe topic of Grit is worth a post because if one can understand its role in fulfilment of one\u2019s goal, one can truly achieve it. Please note though grit is useful only when used in action not in thought. Thinking about mastering a particular programming language over a course of 1 year won\u2019t make you any good at it. Instead, writing few lines of code every single day in the same language and giving some 10min. reading time to its documentation will surely make you a master in no time."},{"id":"/2013/09/26/sing-smile-and-service","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2013/09/26/sing-smile-and-service","source":"@site/blog/2013-09-26-sing-smile-and-service/index.md","title":"Sing, Smile and do Service","description":"What do we really want in life? The spiritualist, the philosopher, the psychologist, the school or college teacher, the affectionate father or a wise elder, all will give you their version of what it means to live. The problem is that none of these answers, however wise they seem really resolve the question. For that\u2026","date":"2013-09-26T18:29:34.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"self development","permalink":"/blog/tags/self-development"},{"inline":true,"label":"embrace the new","permalink":"/blog/tags/embrace-the-new"},{"inline":true,"label":"service","permalink":"/blog/tags/service"}],"readingTime":4.86,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Ajay More","title":"Maintainer of ajaymore.in","url":"https://github.com/ajaymore","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7659804?v=4","key":"ajay","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Sing, Smile and do Service","date":"2013-09-26T18:29:34.000Z","authors":["ajay"],"tags":["self development","embrace the new","service"],"description":"What do we really want in life? The spiritualist, the philosopher, the psychologist, the school or college teacher, the affectionate father or a wise elder, all will give you their version of what it means to live. The problem is that none of these answers, however wise they seem really resolve the question. For that\u2026","image":"./smiley-faces-91.png","mdx":{"format":"md"}},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Grit","permalink":"/blog/2013/10/03/grit"},"nextItem":{"title":"Movies to fuel your imagination","permalink":"/blog/2013/03/23/10-movies-to-fuel-your-imagination"}},"content":"What do we really want in life? The spiritualist, the philosopher, the psychologist, the school or college teacher, the affectionate father or a wise elder, all will give you their version of what it means to live. The problem is that none of these answers, however wise they seem really resolve the question. For that question itself is life. Think about it; this profound question: what do I really want in life? Is all that is there to life? At times it seems like we do know the answer, but we keep coming back to the question, whatever be our age. It gives us a sense of purpose, a vision of destiny.\xa0\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nLife is very dynamic, although we try and believe that everything is linked and there is an underlying order. Deep down we do know that work life can be surprising, painful at times. Many have tried simplifying this randomness of events in life through different ideas. For some it\u2019s a will of God, they believe this apparent chaos is actually order for the controlling & commanding god. For more intellectual types it might be the work of the subconscious mind or a result of collective consciousness of the societies etc. Whatever it is, it really doesn\u2019t matter life is chaotic.\\n\\nI am always fascinated by this dynamism of life events. The inherent uncertainty that surrounds us. Not knowing what might happen. Consider the daily traffic that we pass, countless small or big accidents have happened on that route. From my own driving experience I know for sure that our being a good driver or rider is not sufficient enough to insulate ourselves from a freak accident. The robbery next door in the same apartment, could have happened at our house also. There are so many evil things that happen to innocent people all the time; that sucks! But life works that way. Off course I should take into consideration the beautiful surprises too that happen along the way. A surprise visit by close friends. A sudden wonderful job opportunity that we never dreamed of, or a very rare lottery etc.\\n\\nHowever for untrained minds this randomness is too much. So some rely on religion and believe that their worship will in some way keep them safe and sound. Some Non-believers will confront this randomness of events with a nihilistic idea that there isn\u2019t much too the life any ways. The inquisitive will tackle it with their constant search to find meaning. For me any of this approach is just fine. But none of them make life fun at all. The wisdom that I share below is borrowed from core principals of an organization called Art Of Living. I happen to be an on-off volunteer associated with it. I really love their efforts at reducing all that unnecessary stress in the world. I summarize what I have learnt in three simple words: Sing, Smile & Service.\\n\\nMusic is an integral part of our life and singing is a great stress buster. You don\u2019t need to be singer to sing. Just take some time to hum yourself a nice tune and see how it makes you a pound lighter, not literally \u{1F61B} . So sing by all the means. Some who really don\u2019t like singing, although I don\u2019t see why somebody will hate their own singing voice but still, you can pursue any art that may be more close to your taste. Take up some instrument, or start sketching, or start writing. In short just get yourself an interesting hobby that will make life really worth it. This sums up what I really mean by sing; have something in life that is close to your heart and you don\u2019t really expect any material gains from it.\\n\\n> Wear a smile and have friends; wear a scowl and have wrinkles.  \\n> **George Eliot**\\n\\nThat\u2019s exactly what it means. Smile is very integral to our well-being, and it truly makes you look younger. If you are not the smiley types just one suggestion to you as given by my teacher; \u201Cfake it, until you make it\u201D. A smile in your life will change you forever, it will make you more empathetic. Yes there are some people who appear all smiley and sugary on the outside but are completely rotten and corrupt, but that\u2019s not my point. You keep smiling starting now and see the world lighten up.\\n\\n> I slept and dreamt that life was joy. I awoke and saw that life was service. I acted and behold, service was joy.  \\n> **Rabindranath Tagore**\\n\\nService is most ignored aspect of our life. People find gazillion reasons as to why they can\u2019t do service. For some service only means joining a NGO or some sort of volunteering. Service can be performed anywhere any time. Just be compassionate and have a simple thought. How can I make someone\u2019s day a little easier? From the time you wake up until you sleep, you will get more than a dozen opportunities to help someone. And dozen is just a smallish estimate, you will get many more opportunities if service is a habit for you. Well how does it help you? It\u2019s quite simple, service makes you lose yourself, and your consciousness expands. If I were a neuro-scientist I could tell you exactly what areas in your brain lighten up and which hormone get secreted, but you get the point. It makes you feel awesome. And don\u2019t forget to hold the smile.\\n\\nJust to summarize, remember to Sing, Smile & do Service. Be absolutely selfish about it. It has made my life much easier and might make yours too \u{1F642} . And all that life\u2019s randomness, trust me it won\u2019t bother you. You will show poise when in pain by smiling, you will relieve pain through your service and embrace the unknown through your singing."},{"id":"/2013/03/23/10-movies-to-fuel-your-imagination","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2013/03/23/10-movies-to-fuel-your-imagination","source":"@site/blog/2013-03-23-10-movies-to-fuel-your-imagination/index.md","title":"Movies to fuel your imagination","description":"Below is the list of movies that really change perspective. They are the great examples of how best our imagination can work. I haven\u2019t put them any particular order, but just listed them.","date":"2013-03-23T16:46:21.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Entertainment","permalink":"/blog/tags/entertainment"},{"inline":true,"label":"Movies","permalink":"/blog/tags/movies"}],"readingTime":3.1,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Ajay More","title":"Maintainer of ajaymore.in","url":"https://github.com/ajaymore","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7659804?v=4","key":"ajay","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Movies to fuel your imagination","date":"2013-03-23T16:46:21.000Z","authors":["ajay"],"tags":["Entertainment","Movies"],"description":"Below is the list of movies that really change perspective. They are the great examples of how best our imagination can work. I haven\u2019t put them any particular order, but just listed them.","image":"./animaatjes-lord-of-the-rings-73676-e1342421748170.jpg","mdx":{"format":"md"}},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Sing, Smile and do Service","permalink":"/blog/2013/09/26/sing-smile-and-service"},"nextItem":{"title":"Infosys Mysore","permalink":"/blog/2013/03/23/infosys-mysore"}},"content":"Below is the list of movies that really change perspective. They are the great examples of how best our imagination can work. I haven\u2019t put them any particular order, but just listed them.\xa0\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n### Back to the future\\n\\n![backtothefuture](./backtothefuture.jpg)\\n\\nI have never been able to understand the concept of time travel. Even the book brief history of time by Stephen Hawking hasn\u2019t helped much. However this movie makes time travel absolute fun. It\u2019s pure classic humor, a must watch!\\n\\n### Batman (Dark Knight) Series\\n\\n![dark-knight](./dark-knight.jpg)\\n\\nChristopher Nolan is commercially most successful director and Dark Knight trilogy is the main reason. These movies are intense, thought provoking. An Incorruptible hero (Batman) is an awesome concept. The movies cover all aspects of our emotions. Love, hate, hope, despair, greed, determination.\\n\\n### Star Wars\\nA movie series that fueled the imagination of a generation. Star war movies inspired research into space & technology. The make of this series is a legendary tale. If you wish to know what creativity is then watch star wars from beginning to end.\\n\\n### Harry Potter\\n\\n![HP4D-19625](./harrypotter.jpg)\\n\\nIn a way my generation grew up with Harry Potter. The book is very close to our heart. For a moment I would dream of escaping into the new world of endless possibilities and magic. The movie series is lovely. Hogwarts School of Witchcraft and Wizardry, is an amazing watch.\\n\\n### Jurassic Park\\n\\n![jurassic_Park01](./jurassic_park01.jpg)\\n\\nScience proved that the giant dinosaurs did exist on the planet and perished due to some reason. Some guy thought hmm what would have been a world like if dinosaurs did exist, and we have to live with them. I bet you if you haven\u2019t seen the movie \u2013 you won\u2019t be able to imagine even 0.01% of what\u2019s shown in the movie. The series is phenomenal.\\n\\n### X-men\\n\\n![X-Men-2](./x-men-2.jpg)\\n\\nOne movie where mutation becomes interesting. Guys with special powers. Clashing ideologies. A scared government. X-men is all about style. The concept is awesome and you will enjoy every part. You just can\u2019t miss wolverine.\\n\\n### Matrix\\n\\n![matrix](./matrix.jpg)\\n\\nOne movie I never stop talking about. The theme is open to wide array of interpretation. Just like in matrix our minds are conditioned, the moment we realize this we are on a road to freedom. The movie has lot to teach. More so there are awesome fight sequences, cool sunglasses, martial arts. Beautiful blend of art and science.\\n\\n### Indiana Jones\\n\\n![indiana_jones_13](./indiana_jones_13.jpg)\\n\\nFor those interested in mythology, occult, archeology, history and a hero-villain story this is a must watch series. Harrison ford makes full justice to the demanding role. The movie series is full of surprise, humor and interesting things.\\n\\n### Pirates of the Caribbean\\n\\n![pirate](./pirate.jpg)\\n\\nNothing to be said here. Jack sparrow simply rocks. To think on such a imaginative scale the director must have really visited a madhouse. The movie series is biggest ambassador of unconventionalism. It\u2019s an absolutely new world, a world never imagined.\\n\\n### Transformers\\n\\n![transformers-4-michael-bay](./transformers-4-michael-bay.jpg)\\n\\nCars turning into Robots, I mean even if you can imagine this stuff. How do you show it. The fight sequences in transformers are unbelievably detailed. Makes me wonder, how do these animators keep track of what component of these machines should move at what time when the super fast fights begin.\\n\\n### Lord of the Rings\\n\\n![lotr](./lotr.jpg)\\n\\nKept the best one for the last. Goblins, dwarves, A wizard, hobbits, kings, sorcerers, elves lord of the rings is an inspired creation. The details are awesome. The Middle Earth concept seems so real. Beautiful, breath taking landscapes. Perfect fight sequences. There is enough in the series to keep you interested for eternity."},{"id":"/2013/03/23/infosys-mysore","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2013/03/23/infosys-mysore","source":"@site/blog/2013-03-23-infosys-mysore/index.md","title":"Infosys Mysore","description":"When I first entered the Infosys Mysore campus, I was still a college student. I had come to Mysore campus for my internship. Until then I was a college student, who was used to a typical lifestyle of a hostellite. The luxury of Laundromats, room service, hygienic food, state of the art learning facility and much\u2026","date":"2013-03-23T15:08:59.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Education","permalink":"/blog/tags/education"},{"inline":true,"label":"Infosys","permalink":"/blog/tags/infosys"},{"inline":true,"label":"Paradise","permalink":"/blog/tags/paradise"}],"readingTime":4.7,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Ajay More","title":"Maintainer of ajaymore.in","url":"https://github.com/ajaymore","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7659804?v=4","key":"ajay","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Infosys Mysore","date":"2013-03-23T15:08:59.000Z","authors":["ajay"],"tags":["Education","Infosys","Paradise"],"description":"When I first entered the Infosys Mysore campus, I was still a college student. I had come to Mysore campus for my internship. Until then I was a college student, who was used to a typical lifestyle of a hostellite. The luxury of Laundromats, room service, hygienic food, state of the art learning facility and much\u2026","image":"./dsc09584_1.jpg","mdx":{"format":"md"}},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Movies to fuel your imagination","permalink":"/blog/2013/03/23/10-movies-to-fuel-your-imagination"},"nextItem":{"title":"First Half Marathon","permalink":"/blog/2013/03/23/first-half-marathon"}},"content":"When I first entered the Infosys Mysore campus, I was still a college student. I had come to Mysore campus for my internship. Until then I was a college student, who was used to a typical lifestyle of a hostellite. The luxury of Laundromats, room service, hygienic\xa0food, state of the art learning facility and much more\xa0was something never heard of. The Mysore campus came as quite a surprise for all of us. From the moment I entered the campus until this date I can\u2019t stop talking about it. The place is definition of beauty, aesthetics, modernization, discipline and wonder.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n![MY DC](./dsc09584_1.jpg)\\n*MY DC*\\n\\nTo write the entire Mysore training story will take pages, and I will still be left with so much to tell. In this post I will simply make note of what I loved when I lived there. I must really appreciate the efforts of Infosys founders and all those who were involved in building this monumental campus. The campus has given a reason for thousands to appreciate something and dream big in their life. It has generated valuable jobs for the people of Mysore.\\n\\n![Roman architecture](./dsc09602_1.jpg)\\n*Roman architecture*\\n\\nThe campus is an architectural masterpiece. Every building is part of a design, and just doesn\u2019t stand there. The colossal GECII inspired by Roman architecture, the origami building, The Multiplex are nothing less than a work of art. The campus has 8 food courts, each food court is unique, lovely and grand. They even have beautiful names. An aerial view of the campus will show the name Infosys where hostels are located. The hostels are located in such a fashion so as to create the name Infosys. There is greenery everywhere, nothing is out of place. One can even spot several birds in the campus.\\n\\nOne thing that I loved most than any other was my room. The huge window, an awesome sofa and a cup of coffee was an irresistible combination. There was more than sufficient furniture. I still miss the cozy bed, the Raymond blanket and silky smooth bed spread. Had an LCD TV all for myself which was quite frankly all I could have asked for. The bathroom was equally beautiful and provided hot water in the morning. :)The best part was we could order a pizza using the intercom and be as lazy as we would want. My room even had a wooden flooring; That was just way to rich.\\n\\n![DSC09202](./dsc09202-e1398239230146.jpg)\\n\\nOne gets friends of a life time at Mysore campus. Mysore days are days of celebration and celebration is incomplete without friends. Everyone enjoys and suffers the same condition at the campus and that makes friendship all the more powerful. The strict rules of the campus calls up for many adventures which would be impossible without help from your close friends. The adventures are a plenty and would take a whole new blog post. All days at the campus are full of fun, humor, adventure, satire, wonderful crushes and much more.\\n\\n![DSC09379_1](./dsc09379_1.jpg)\\n\\nI knew how to swim, but I still wouldn\u2019t call myself a swimmer. Here I learnt proper strokes with help of a trainer. In the end could swim 25mts underwater, and later at one occasion even swam 2000mts in a single go. The extra-curricular activities in the campus is the biggest attraction. There is in-house bowling alley, Bouldering Wall, basketball, volleyball, badminton court, Football ground surrounded by synthetic jogging track. Cricket ground, pool, carrom, lawn tennis, squash, table tennis, a gym facility, yoga room and many more.\xa0It\u2019s kind of you name it and we have it. More so everything is state of the art facility.\\n\\n![Multiplex](./dsc09369_1.jpg)\\n*Multiplex*\\n\\nThe entire facility is called ECC \u2013 Employee Care Center. There is a multiplex inside the campus which plays all the latest movies. Then there is a small retail store called Loyal World which caters to most of the shopping needs of the residents. There is everything one can ask for, and some more. It was left with every individual how to make judicious use of all these facilities. If one wishes to learn what quality is? Then the person should come to this place. Be it lawn-mowing at cricket ground, or the quality of plumbing used at various places, quality is not compromised for any reason. The house keeping staff literally washes the streets to keep it clean and beautiful.\\n\\n![DSC09434_1](./dsc09434_1.jpg)\\n\\nThe campus is meant to be house for thousands of people who will come and go. So it is of utmost importance to provide such a facility which will last for very long. The house keeping takes the motto \u201Cprevention is better than cure\u201D very seriously. Every room, every corner, every door is meticulously cleaned day in day out. So as to avoid any unforeseeable complication.\\n\\n![DSC09567_1](./dsc09567_1.jpg)\\n\\nAll glamor and glitz apart the campus is a place for exceptional world class training facility. The class rooms are simply amazing. The quality of trainers and the training material is equally amazing. If I were to really make a statement, I would say, all the coding that I can do today. I learnt it here. Thanks to all my friends for their great support when I was as dumb as anyone could be. I couldn\u2019t even write a basic SQL query in the beginning, but the training process and a little hard work paid off. Mysore days were truly the best days of my life and will cherish them forever. Thanks to all my batch mates, you guys made it special.\\n\\n![DSC09543](./dsc09543.jpg)"},{"id":"/2013/03/23/first-half-marathon","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2013/03/23/first-half-marathon","source":"@site/blog/2013-03-23-first-half-marathon/index.md","title":"First Half Marathon","description":"Physical activity is an important aspect of our life. As we age we give less and less importance to this aspect under the pretext of priorities. I decided to run a marathon to provide myself a reminder for the rest of my life. A reminder of being mentally and physically strong, a reason to start\u2026","date":"2013-03-23T10:40:20.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Physical Performance","permalink":"/blog/tags/physical-performance"},{"inline":true,"label":"Determination","permalink":"/blog/tags/determination"},{"inline":true,"label":"Half Marathon","permalink":"/blog/tags/half-marathon"}],"readingTime":4.49,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Ajay More","title":"Maintainer of ajaymore.in","url":"https://github.com/ajaymore","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7659804?v=4","key":"ajay","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"First Half Marathon","date":"2013-03-23T10:40:20.000Z","authors":["ajay"],"tags":["Physical Performance","Determination","Half Marathon"],"description":"Physical activity is an important aspect of our life. As we age we give less and less importance to this aspect under the pretext of priorities. I decided to run a marathon to provide myself a reminder for the rest of my life. A reminder of being mentally and physically strong, a reason to start\u2026","image":"./half-marathon.png","mdx":{"format":"md"}},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Infosys Mysore","permalink":"/blog/2013/03/23/infosys-mysore"},"nextItem":{"title":"Divinity Of Numbers","permalink":"/blog/2013/02/27/divinity-of-numbers"}},"content":"Physical activity is an important aspect of our life. As we age we give less and less importance to this aspect under the pretext of priorities. I decided to run a marathon to provide myself a reminder for the rest of my life. A reminder of being mentally and physically strong, a reason to start working out again whenever I lose touch. To do it at least once so that it will inspire me for a lifetime. My inspiration was my best friend who in spite of a knee injury and a one year rest to recover from subsequent surgery came out strong and ran his first half marathon.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nAlthough I registered for the marathon, the work kept me busy. It was difficult finding time for training. I was working out after quite a long time and suffered few injuries in the course of training too. The real preparation from my side I can say, was only buying a pair of beautiful Adidas jogging shoes.\\n\\nA day prior to marathon, I had to go to office due to an urgent requirement. After finishing my work I traveled from Pune to Mumbai. Reached Mumbai around 10:30pm for a stay at friend\u2019s place. The other two of my buddies also arrived. We had a heavy dinner and we kept chatting until 1:00am in the morning. We were to report at the marathon venue at 5:30am in the morning, so even if we were to wake up at 4:30 in the morning we would have had only 3 and half hours of sleep. At this point I was getting bugged by the thought of not having taken adequate rest. By the morning I had my own doubts if I could even make it.\\n\\nWe reached in time to the venue. Had our luggage secured at the luggage desk. Then the marathon started without any delay. I had a only goal in mind to go as slow as possible and to complete the marathon. Even if I were to walk the entire distance which I could have done easily I could do it in 3 hours, so thought to myself, yes this is possible. As the running progressed, I found myself more comfortable and started to gradually increase my pace. As I started to overtake few of the fellow marathoners my confidence grew. There were people on the streets cheering, volunteers providing water and lemon juice. Feeling was special.\\n\\nAfter a very long run, saw a board with a bold 9km on it. I had not run a marathon before, so the sign was free for my misinterpretation. I thought I was only left with 9kms now. \u{1F642} As I misread the signboard, I increased my pace again, I was under the impression it were only a matter of 9kms now, and I must have already completed 12kms. From then on after a long tiresome run with increased pace, saw a new signboard with bold 15km, and my hopes were dashed. Took me no time to realize that back then I had completed only 9kms. Now the pace dropped, It was brutal from this moment.\\n\\nThe last 6kms were absolutely excruciating. Although my mind was ready, my legs couldn\u2019t support the stress. There were moments when I wondered at my pace and thought even a walker by will overtake me at this speed. More than 100 marathoners ran past me in this stretch. Few ran alongside me and cheered me to push a little and I did. I could have stopped midway, but the all-powerful mind kept persistent. I pushed myself to the limit. As I look at it now, it was quite naive. I was exposing myself to some serious injury. But that had never been a concern ever for me. Suffered cramps in last 1km and almost tripped at one point. All in all I was very glad to complete the run in 2hours 9 minutes.\\n\\nOne should have this experience at least once in life. The participants are special, the half marathon participants are mostly office going men and women who take time from their daily routine to take care of their physique too. They train themselves in spite of challenges put forth by their working lifestyles. It feels great to be among such inspired crowd. All special thanks to the organizing committee. To arrange such a huge event requires tremendous planning, infrastructure and volunteers. The whole experience inspires me to prepare for the next big run. There is an ecstasy of overcoming your limitations, pride of achievement. A stronger sense of self belief.\\n\\nI should not forget to mention, training for such events is absolutely essential. Only mental preparedness is not enough. There was a fatality in the event I ran, and that should not happen to anybody. The participants should really work on the ground realities before signing up for such events. There is always a dream run of 8kms for the folks who do not wish to experience the strain put forth by half and full marathon. For 3 days post marathon it was difficult for me to even walk. I couldn\u2019t climb or descend the stairs without the support of railings. I was lucky I didn\u2019t suffer any lasting injury, but a lesson was learnt. Next time I will be more prepared both mentally and physically to meet the challenge."},{"id":"/2013/02/27/divinity-of-numbers","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2013/02/27/divinity-of-numbers","source":"@site/blog/2013-02-27-divinity-of-numbers/index.md","title":"Divinity Of Numbers","description":"If any subject has given better solutions to our relentless Meta physical questions regarding life and it\u2019s manifestations, there is none better than Mathematics. Mathematicians for over thousand years have been coming up with abstract mathematical equations which may or may not really hold any relevance to our lives as such. Even though these formulas\u2026","date":"2013-02-27T13:50:46.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Leisure","permalink":"/blog/tags/leisure"},{"inline":true,"label":"Mathematics","permalink":"/blog/tags/mathematics"}],"readingTime":2.17,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Ajay More","title":"Maintainer of ajaymore.in","url":"https://github.com/ajaymore","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7659804?v=4","key":"ajay","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Divinity Of Numbers","date":"2013-02-27T13:50:46.000Z","authors":["ajay"],"tags":["Leisure","Mathematics"],"description":"If any subject has given better solutions to our relentless Meta physical questions regarding life and it\u2019s manifestations, there is none better than Mathematics. Mathematicians for over thousand years have been coming up with abstract mathematical equations which may or may not really hold any relevance to our lives as such. Even though these formulas\u2026","image":"./800px-fibonacci_spiral1.png","mdx":{"format":"md"}},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"First Half Marathon","permalink":"/blog/2013/03/23/first-half-marathon"},"nextItem":{"title":"Surya Namaskar","permalink":"/blog/2012/12/03/surya-namaskar"}},"content":"If any subject has given better solutions to our relentless Meta physical questions regarding life and it\u2019s manifestations, there is none better than Mathematics. Mathematicians for over thousand years have been coming up with abstract mathematical equations which may or may not really hold any relevance to our lives as such. Even though these formulas or equations were created in a different time-frame and without any material base, it\u2019s interesting how they perfectly fit into definition of our daily lives.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nOne mathematician I particularly like is Pythagoras. The first 4 numbers defined in Pythagoras tradition, are pretty much capable of describing the entire world.\\n\\nThe dot or number 1 represent the beginning of the universe. It\u2019s the first transition from unknown to the known.  \\nThe line created by connecting 2 dots gives birth to number 2 and the one dimensional reality.  \\nThe triangle or any plane can be defined by three points not lying on a single line. The number 3 gives birth to the two dimensional reality.  \\nThe number four, which can be considered combination of above three numbers defines the 3-dimensional reality into which we reside.\\n\\nOur entire understanding of the world can be described using these 4 numbers alone. An entirely new number system can be found using number 4 as base. It\u2019s interesting how mathematics can so accurately define our life, although its subject matter was not necessarily created with the purpose of making any material sense at all. We cannot prove the right angle triangle equation using physical objects as it\u2019s impossible to create perfect right angle triangle, still the equation holds true and is part of our everyday solutions.\\n\\nThe famous Right angle triangle equation, the fabled irrational number pi (22/7) which defines all the circles, the Fibonacci series which defines the pattern in a sea shell, or the formation of leaves on some trees, the probability theory which defines a lot of our life, and also the activity at sub-atomic level, beautiful equations\xa0 which define the planetary moments, also the activity of entire cosmos as such. Though these formulas weren\u2019t created with the purpose of defining materialism, the material reality seems to perfectly fit into their definition.\\n\\nLet me confess I don\u2019t understand much of mathematics. However there is something about this subject that has left me in such awe and wonder. Like music it doesn\u2019t matter if I don\u2019t understand the process of creation of mathematical entities. I know the taste, I know how the world around me has been transformed as result of its application. I can almost certainly enjoy it. \u{1F642}"},{"id":"/2012/12/03/surya-namaskar","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2012/12/03/surya-namaskar","source":"@site/blog/2012-12-03-surya-namaskar/index.md","title":"Surya Namaskar","description":"From improving your posture, strengthening muscles to whittling extra inches around the waist, the benefits of Suryanamaskar are many, provided you adapt it the right way. A set of 12 fixed, cyclic postures define Suryanamaskar which when performed repeatedly at an easy pace can bring a sense of wellbeing, almost immediately. However, those with a\u2026","date":"2012-12-03T14:56:46.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Health","permalink":"/blog/tags/health"},{"inline":true,"label":"Yoga","permalink":"/blog/tags/yoga"}],"readingTime":1.3,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Ajay More","title":"Maintainer of ajaymore.in","url":"https://github.com/ajaymore","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7659804?v=4","key":"ajay","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Surya Namaskar","date":"2012-12-03T14:56:46.000Z","authors":["ajay"],"tags":["Health","Yoga"],"description":"From improving your posture, strengthening muscles to whittling extra inches around the waist, the benefits of Suryanamaskar are many, provided you adapt it the right way. A set of 12 fixed, cyclic postures define Suryanamaskar which when performed repeatedly at an easy pace can bring a sense of wellbeing, almost immediately. However, those with a\u2026","mdx":{"format":"md"}},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Divinity Of Numbers","permalink":"/blog/2013/02/27/divinity-of-numbers"},"nextItem":{"title":"Art of Living Yes+ Advance Course","permalink":"/blog/2012/12/03/art-of-living-yes-advance-course"}},"content":"From improving your posture, strengthening muscles to whittling extra inches around the waist, the benefits of Suryanamaskar are many, provided you adapt it the right way. A set of 12 fixed, cyclic postures define Suryanamaskar which when performed repeatedly at an easy pace can bring a sense of wellbeing, almost immediately. However, those with a heart condition, arthritis or slip-disk, need their doctor\u2019s consent before starting the routine. If you have been gravely out of form in a way that you haven\u2019t stretched your muscles in ages, first give your body some time to open up, which you can do with some flexibility and stamina-building exercises before embarking on the more arduous \u2018Suryanamaskar\u2019.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nAnd once your body has registered a certain fitness level, you can begin with a set of three Suryanamaskars in the first instance and increase it to five then ten and more depending on your stamina. Any more than clocking up numbers, it is important to get each posture right, for the very essence of this yogic ritual lies in perfecting every move. To say the least, it can be an uphill task for beginners. But our expert-backed warm-up exercises are sure to make Suryanamaskar less strenuous and ever so graceful.\\n\\n<iframe src=\\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/gQn-QHLqNvI\\" width=\\"640\\" height=\\"360\\" style=\\"max-width: 100%; border: 0;\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" allowfullscreen></iframe>\\n\\nBenefits I have experienced.\\n\\n1.  Graceful posture\\n2.  Fluid body movements\\n3.  Improved Stamina\\n4.  Perfect body balance and better reflexes\\n5.  Improved core strength\\n6.  Better breath control"},{"id":"/2012/12/03/art-of-living-yes-advance-course","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2012/12/03/art-of-living-yes-advance-course","source":"@site/blog/2012-12-03-art-of-living-yes-advance-course/index.md","title":"Art of Living Yes+ Advance Course","description":"Last winter I visited the Art of living foundation\u2019s Banglore ashram for a spiritual retreat. It was a course meant for youngsters from all over the world. I wasn\u2019t attending such a course for a very first time but still I felt quite anxious about it. Sometimes I had to rethink my decision of going\u2026","date":"2012-12-03T08:45:13.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"self development","permalink":"/blog/tags/self-development"},{"inline":true,"label":"Art Of Living","permalink":"/blog/tags/art-of-living"}],"readingTime":5.34,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Ajay More","title":"Maintainer of ajaymore.in","url":"https://github.com/ajaymore","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7659804?v=4","key":"ajay","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Art of Living Yes+ Advance Course","date":"2012-12-03T08:45:13.000Z","authors":["ajay"],"tags":["self development","Art Of Living"],"description":"Last winter I visited the Art of living foundation\u2019s Banglore ashram for a spiritual retreat. It was a course meant for youngsters from all over the world. I wasn\u2019t attending such a course for a very first time but still I felt quite anxious about it. Sometimes I had to rethink my decision of going\u2026","image":"./yes_04.jpg","mdx":{"format":"md"}},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Surya Namaskar","permalink":"/blog/2012/12/03/surya-namaskar"},"nextItem":{"title":"25 mts underwater and a smoked cigarette","permalink":"/blog/2012/11/04/cigarette-n-swimming"}},"content":"Last winter I visited the Art of living foundation\u2019s Banglore ashram for a spiritual retreat. It was a course meant for youngsters from all over the world. I wasn\u2019t attending such a course for a very first time but still I felt quite anxious about it. Sometimes I had to rethink my decision of going all the way to Banglore just to meditate and be in silence. Now when I remember it I really feel glad that I did go there. However progressive we might be but it\u2019s still quite difficult to embrace the new. I had to make quite an effort to let myself believe that I was doing the right thing.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nThe train journey from Mumbai to Banglore itself was quite an experience in itself. I had always been thankful to AOL for helping me let go of most of my inhibitions. The train journey was a time for letting go of few many more. Although I am quite good at it I usually don\u2019t converse much with people on purpose. But here it was an entirely a different thing. I had to make absolutely no effort to get acquainted with most people on the train. The participants for the course were very enthusiastic about the journey. They had thought of many games, there was a lot of stuff happening. I had no choice but to include myself in all the enjoyment that was happening around me. I had been cynical about all that was happening for a while but in no time I got involved and I would say that was the beginning of the most beautiful experience in my life. We sang, we laughed, we teased, and we played and did all sorts of things that were imaginable.\\n\\nOnce inside the Ashram I was welcomed into a new world. Isolated from chaos and gloom of daily life, this place felt like heaven. The forest like nature of the place, beautiful and magnificent architectures with a perfect blend of modern and ancient aesthetics. So perfectly organized was the place yet there was no trace of monotony that is usually involved in organization. The cool, serene lake made all my defenses fall down. I was left with so much awe and wonder that I must say I wasn\u2019t me at all. There were many constructions happening at the same time and the commitment of the people towards making those structures and the love with which they did their work was a sure indicator of what wonder was going to be created. The natural uneven hilly terrain has given a very exotic look to the whole place. There was every aspect of modernization out there and yet it felt like having come to a very remote natural haven.\\n\\nThe course was off course a huge success. Every aspect of it was pre planned and meticulously carried out. The course participants were in hundreds and yet the course went uninterrupted until the end. A lot of management consisted of volunteers and yet there were hardly any communication gaps that usually occur when working with volunteers. I was totally amazed at the way things were happening; it was so much beyond my comprehension. The regular laws of management didn\u2019t seem to work there, there was something magical about it. The food was the most exotic thing one could find in this place. It was so healthy and yet so tasty which is quite uncharacteristic of a healthy food. The very action of eating such a food gave me a feeling of cleansing.  \\nThe course was meant to be an absolute fun and it proved to be so. The entire structure of the course was based on the premise that best of the education can only be achieved through an informal and active teaching. So there we were absolutely informal and learning meditation. We learnt few yogic postures to be done in a sequence for a better experience. We played many little games which taught us few valuable lessons in life. We had a brief period of absolute silence lasting 2 days which helped us have an insight into our inner selves and know for real what we were. The period of silence made me realize the futility of communication. It made us realize how we took our power of expression for granted and have always failed in achieving a better communication because of that. There were few lectures by eminent personalities as an icing on the cake. The course took care of refining almost all the aspect of our being. The meditation purified the mind, the food purified the body, and the yoga unified the body, mind and soul. The lectures added an intellectual knowledge.\\n\\nThe time with the participants was the most awesome experience of my life. Out there everyone was so selfless, so unpretentious, so natural, so loving and so caring. The energy in the ashram had changed everybody. There was a smile and gratitude on every face, an unusual innocence influencing their every action. The glow of happiness on everybody\u2019s face, and I wondered who would not want to be with such people. There was so much of enthusiasm, such a high commitment and no expectations. The satsangs were the most energizing experiences. We sang to our hearts and danced beyond our abilities. It was a very playful environment. We laughed until we choked and we laughed more. We played until we got tired and we played a little more. We meditated until we got absolutely bored and we meditated more. We smiled until very appearance of our faces changed and we smiled a bit more. We sang until our vocal cords gave up and we sang beautifully, we danced until our minds gave up and we danced better.\\n\\nThe experience was one of its kind, away from greed, lust, anger and jealousy that define our very life \u2013 it was a heavenly experience. There was no gender difference, there was no bias, we hugged each other, we shared, and we cared. It made me realize how beautiful my life was. It made me grateful by multitudes of millions towards the creation. I thought many more should have this experience but seldom people understand beyond limitations of the language. This was my little effort to describe beauty which in itself is a nonsensical effort. Beauty must be experienced and until we do experience we never realize\u2026"},{"id":"/2012/11/04/cigarette-n-swimming","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/2012/11/04/cigarette-n-swimming","source":"@site/blog/2012-11-04-cigarette-n-swimming/index.md","title":"25 mts underwater and a smoked cigarette","description":"I used to smoke on rare occasions. I started smoking as a result of my curiosity to try new things and in order to fit into my pear group. It was a perfectly monitored undertaking, I would make sure I don\u2019t smoke on more than 4-5 occasions in a month. If there was a slightest\u2026","date":"2012-11-04T11:45:12.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Leisure","permalink":"/blog/tags/leisure"},{"inline":true,"label":"Cigarette","permalink":"/blog/tags/cigarette"}],"readingTime":2.27,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Ajay More","title":"Maintainer of ajaymore.in","url":"https://github.com/ajaymore","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7659804?v=4","key":"ajay","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"25 mts underwater and a smoked cigarette","date":"2012-11-04T11:45:12.000Z","authors":["ajay"],"tags":["Leisure","Cigarette"],"description":"I used to smoke on rare occasions. I started smoking as a result of my curiosity to try new things and in order to fit into my pear group. It was a perfectly monitored undertaking, I would make sure I don\u2019t smoke on more than 4-5 occasions in a month. If there was a slightest\u2026","mdx":{"format":"md"}},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Art of Living Yes+ Advance Course","permalink":"/blog/2012/12/03/art-of-living-yes-advance-course"}},"content":"I used to smoke on rare occasions. I started smoking as a result of my curiosity to try new things and in order to fit into my pear group. It was a perfectly monitored undertaking, I would make sure I don\u2019t smoke on more than 4-5 occasions in a month. If there was a slightest doubt that I was starting to like it then I would simply stop smoking. Today I have quite a many practical reasons to avoid smoking and my attitude towards smokers is not hostile like many non-smokers.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nOne particular incident about smoking that I always share is when I swam 25mts underwater in a single breath, the inspiration came from a smoked cigarette. One night I was just sitting idle in my company hostel\u2019s smoking zone. I had a cigarette in my mouth. Since there was no one to talk with, or anything worthwhile to do, all I was left with was watching myself smoke. As I was watched, I began to consciously take big puffs and hold it in my lung for a longer time. Then I slowly exhaled, as slow as possible. Not to mention the kick was awesome. But I began to realize the inhaling and exhaling process was similar to my underwater swimming trials.\\n\\nUntil that time I was really struggling with underwater swimming. The breath was hard to handle. I would take a big gulp of air and go under water but in few seconds when I tried to release the air in my lungs. The lungs would empty themselves and I would be soon left with no breath at all. All my attempt at slow release weren\u2019t really working out. For some or other reason I couldn\u2019t keep a check over my breath exhalation under water. I couldn\u2019t keep my exhalation steady.\\n\\nBut here in smoking zone. I could actually see the air exiting my lungs, and now I had a far better control. Since I could see I could easily control the flow. I tried to release the toxic fume as slow and steady as possible. After about 2-3 cigarettes I was pretty confident, if I apply the same technique under water, I might go a farther distance. The next day I tried the same, and was pretty successful.\\n\\n25mts underwater is not a big deal. And smoking is not cool at all. It really destroys your body. I just thought of sharing a peculiar incident and I would be grateful if you drop any ideas that might have occurred to you in course of reading this post. Having said that I will encourage you to experiment with life, take chances. You are the maker of your own destiny."}]}}')}}]);